  precompiled header built at stage time follows the flags of the stage; a
  run with CXXFLAGS set builds its own member at its first compile.  CI
  pays the Prelude compile in the make stage of every job now (about 30 s),
  which the first test import paid before; the py job pays it too.
- Content-keyed ICurry cache (issue #12).  The Curry-to-ICurry cache was off
  by default and could not hit for a dynamic module: its table was named
  after CURRYPATH, which starts with a fresh temporary directory for every
  curry.compile, and its key held the module name, which curry.compile
  numbers per process.  So every unit test paid the front end (about 5 s per
  module; unit_compile.py: 21 calls, 128 s on the Python backend).  Done:
  the key (cache.icurry_cache_key) is a SHA-256 of the module source, the
  sources of the modules it imports (transitively, found as .curry files in
  the Curry path and beside the file; the Prelude is implicit; a module that
  is nowhere, such as a library of PAKCS, adds nothing), the front-end
  options (none today), the icurry tool itself, and the intermediate
  subdirectory, which names the library version.  The file name is not part
  of the key.  The module name is part of it for a named module, because the
  front end writes it into the ICurry; an anonymous module
  (sprite__interactive_N, sprite__expression_N; config.is_anonymous_modname)
  leaves it out, and the cached text is renamed on a hit
  (cache.rename_module: the name follows a double quote or a hash sign and
  precedes a double quote or a dot; verified against the front end's own
  output for another name on a module with instances, a superclass, a
  default method, lambdas, local functions, and an external: 133
  occurrences, equal byte for byte).  A program error of the front end (an
  error at a source position) is cached too and replayed as the same
  CompileError with the new name; a failure of the environment is not
  cached.  SPRITE_CACHE_FILE now names the file and turns the cache on; the
  empty string turns it off; unset, Make.config decides as before.  The test
  drivers (tests/run_tests and the CI script) set tests/.cache/icurry.db,
  and the workflow restores and saves tests/.cache with actions/cache
  (restore keys: the same job, then any job; saved after the tests even when
  one failed).  SPRITE_CACHE_UPDATE matches the Curry source file name.
  The rows live in table curry2icurry_2 (key, name, text, error, created);
  a database error is a warning and the front end runs.  The parsed-JSON
  cache is unchanged.  Found on the way: _system.pexec now attaches
  command, returncode, and stderr to its CompileError.  Measured on the
  Python backend, CPU time of the test process and its children:
  unit_compile.py cold with the cache: 11 tests, 21 front end calls,
  143.7 s user and 42.6 s system (the machine was shared; the same file
  without the cache took 94.6 s user and 33.4 s system earlier in the day,
  21 calls as well); the second run: 0 front end calls, 21 hits (one of
  them the replayed program error), 0.67 s user and 0.09 s system, 0.6 s of
  wall time.  A hit costs about 0.01 s of CPU in curry.compile; the key
  alone costs 2.3 ms (it reads and digests the source and its import
  closure every time: four library modules, 87 KB, for an expression that
  imports Data.List and Control.SetFunctions), and the digest of the icurry
  tool 2 ms once per process.  Tests: unit_cache.py (the rename, the key
  and the import closure, the slot without the front end, the error replay,
  curry.compile hits in the process and in a second process, a named module
  through the toolchain).  Left: the import scan
  is a regular expression over import declarations, lenient on purpose; a
  module present only as .icy or .json.z adds nothing to the key; entries
  never expire (delete the file); the PAKCS version is covered by the
  subdirectory, not by a digest of PAKCS; the old tables of an existing
  ~/.sprite/cache.db stay in the file.
- Bytecode cache for generated Python modules (issue #13).  The prebuild
  entry above covers the Prelude and the system modules: the toolchain
  writes the cache when it writes a .py file, and the loader reads it
  through importlib.  Left open there: a generated file without a cache,
  such as a test module from the overlay archive or from an installation
  made before the cache was written, was compiled from source in every
  process, because the test drivers run Python with -B and importlib writes
  no cache then (the 1,151 generated test modules under tests/data had no
  __pycache__ at all).  Done: the loader writes a missing or stale cache
  (ensure_bytecode) before it loads the file, -B or not; the cache is a
  product of the toolchain, not of an import.  So a generated module is
  compiled from source at most once per change, in any process.  A cache
  that cannot be written gives one warning and the file loads from source.
  Measured on the Python backend, new processes, CPU time: the Prelude
  import costs 0.026 to 0.035 s with zero source compiles (import curry
  0.064 to 0.076 s before it).  unit_py_evaluation.py with a warm ICurry
  cache, CPU of the test process after the imports, whole process in
  parentheses (user plus system): 1.23 s (1.34 to 1.35 s) with the loader of
  the committed tree, which ran the file through runpy and compiled the
  Prelude six times (once per hard reset) and each test module once; 0.26
  to 0.27 s (0.37 s) with the cache of the Prelude alone, the five test
  modules still compiled in every process; 0.32 s (0.43 s) in the run that
  wrote their caches and 0.28 to 0.29 s (0.41 to 0.42 s) after it, zero
  source compiles of cached files.  The five modules are small (1 to 5 KB),
  so their compile cost is in the noise of that file; the gain of the loader
  change is that the larger test modules and the 1,151 modules of the
  functional suites are compiled once instead of once per process.  Tests:
  unit_prebuild.py (the loader writes a stale or missing cache under -B, a
  file without a cache is compiled by the first of two child processes only,
  an unwritable cache gives one warning through the loader).  Left: a
  generated .py still carries no format stamp (see the prebuild entry); a
  read-only tree whose caches are missing warns at every load.
- Run statistics (issue #8).  sprite-exec -t printed the wall time of the
  evaluation at 1 ms resolution and nothing else, and the counters of the
  runtimes (steps_total and gc_collections of the C++ runtime, the step
  counter and the telemetry object of the Python backend) reached no tool.
  Done: sprite-exec --stats prints one line of key=value pairs on stderr
  when the program ends, after its output and after an error message:
  wall (seconds since the process started, read from /proc on Linux, since
  the import of the package elsewhere), cpu (user plus system seconds of
  the process, children excluded), steps (rewrite steps), forks (forks of
  a choice-rooted configuration), collections (the node collector of the
  C++ backend; 0 on the Python backend), peak_rss (bytes, from getrusage),
  and compile (seconds spent in the steps of the toolchain: front end,
  JSON, code generator, C++ compiler; 0 when every file was current).  The
  same numbers come from curry.stats() (Interpreter.stats, module
  interpreter/stats.py): a dict with the seven keys in that order whose
  str is the line.  Steps and forks are per interpreter: the runtime state
  of an evaluation counts its own (steps_total and forks_total on both
  backends; forks_total is new in the C++ RuntimeState, counted in fork),
  and the generic evaluator sums them in EvaluationTotals: a running
  evaluation is held by a weak reference and counted as it goes; an
  evaluation whose generator ends or is dropped is added once.  No hot
  path changed on either backend.  The compile clock sits around the step
  loop of makecurry (outermost call only), so an import that finds every
  file current adds nothing.  The other fields are per process.  Measured
  on mynot (committed caches, no front end): the Python backend reports
  steps=3 forks=1 and the C++ backend steps=4 forks=1 (the backends count
  differently; compare steps within one backend); the first C++ run after
  the header change compiled mynot.so again and reported compile=0.62 s,
  the next run compile=0.000000; cpu about 0.12 s on both backends,
  peak_rss 35 to 37 MB.  Tests: unit_api.py (TestStats: the fields and
  the line, the counts of an evaluation and of a running one, the compile
  field on a hand-written module and on a current one, sprite-exec --stats
  after a run and after an error, no line without the option),
  unit_py_interp.py (testStats: totals per interpreter, kept across a soft
  reset), unit_cxx_gc.py (the collections field follows gc_collections).
  Left: a direct step through evaluator.single_step is added as well, but
  not tested; the harness (M1) is the consumer of the line.
