- Plain-data module format (L5, issue #18).  With the precompiled header a
  small module still cost g++ about 0.3 s for its static Metadata maps
  (std::unordered_map of std::variant) and its ModuleBOM aggregate
  (std::string, std::vector, std::map, std::tuple): the instantiation of the
  templates and the code of the dynamic initializers.  Done: the generated
  code spells the bill of materials as static arrays of plain records (new
  header cyrt/bom.hpp, namespace cyrt::bom: Entry, Metadata, Alias, Type,
  Function, Module; every member is an integer, a pointer, or a string
  literal) and exports one record, _bom_.  The record is declared extern
  inside the extern "C" block, because a const object at namespace scope has
  internal linkage otherwise.  The compiler writes the arrays as data: no
  template is instantiated for them and no constructor runs at load.  The
  loader (SharedCurryModule) reads the record, refuses one of another layout
  version (bom::VERSION; the DynloadError names both versions; a library
  without the record and a record without a name are errors too), and
  decodes it into the ModuleBOM the runtime and the bindings read
  (ModuleBOM::decode).  The registry entry owns the decoded object.  A
  metadata record shared between symbols is decoded once (the Prelude shares
  36 records among about 1,700 symbols).  Module::link, loader.py, and the
  Python view of the bill of materials are unchanged.  FORMAT_VERSION 3 -> 4:
  a format-3 file no longer compiles against the new headers (the first
  member of ModuleBOM is now the deque of the decoded metadata), and the
  stamp keeps it from the compiler.  The ABI stamp is unchanged; the header
  change recompiles every object once.  The emitter refuses a metadata value
  that is not a str, an int, or a bool, and an int outside the C++ range,
  with a CompileError that names the key (g++ failed on such a value
  before).  The symbol kind MODULE_DATA ('T') names the tables.  Measured on
  the C++ backend: g++ on Fib.cpp with the precompiled header 0.62 -> 0.35 s
  user (0.73 -> 0.44 s wall); -ftime-report: opt and generate 0.55 -> 0.27 s,
  template instantiation 0.20 -> 0.15 s.  Peano takes 0.54 s user (0.64 s
  wall) after the change: its 15 step functions cost 0.47 s in opt and
  generate at -O3.  The Prelude 27.4 -> 26.5 s user (its 3,194 step
  functions take almost all of it).  Compile suite (-r 3, CPU seconds of the
  whole process): Peano warm 1.230 -> 0.855 (0.70), Fib warm 0.918 -> 0.642
  (0.70), cold 0.83 and 0.79; the compile field: Peano warm 1.103 -> 0.737
  (0.67), Fib warm 0.793 -> 0.530 (0.67).  func_eqconstr.py on the C++
  backend with every module regenerated and compiled again (767 programs;
  CPU seconds of the test process and its children; five chunks of the
  file, one process each): 569.5 -> 318.8 s (0.56).  Throughput (seven
  programs, -r 3): every item the same within 3 percent, counters equal;
  the import suite the same.  Static evidence: the module objects (the test
  module and the staged Prelude.so) import no operator new or delete, no
  _Hashtable, and no basic_string; before, every module imported operator
  new for its maps.  Tests: unit_cxx_bom.py (the decoded bill of materials
  of data/curry/CxxBom.curry, with a metadata value of each kind and a
  private function; the generated file; the undefined symbols of the
  objects, the Prelude included; the loader's checks on hand-compiled
  libraries; the emitter's errors).  Left: g++ now spends its time on the
