- Literal and small-value tables on the C++ backend (T5).  Generated code
  allocated a boxed literal on every execution of a step (Tak1 three per
  recursing call, Fib two, the Prelude's length one per element).  Done: the
  runtime keeps one node for each integer from -128 to 1023 and for each
  ASCII character (g_small_ints and g_small_chars in builtins.hpp), made
  when the library loads.  The literal nodes live in an arena of their own
  (literal_reserve in graph/memory.hpp, one block of 16 MB committed as it
  is touched), outside the heap of the collector: the mark phase skips them
  by their address, and the sweep never sees them, so a collection costs
  nothing for them.  A first version registered them as roots, which the
  stress mode exposed: a collection on a heap of two live nodes took 4.9 us,
  all of it for the 1,292 roots, and the walk of a million cells inside a
  set function in unit_cxx_gc.py no longer finished in its 120 s.  int_ and
  char_ return the shared node of a value in range and allocate otherwise;
  float_ is unchanged.  The debug flavor asserts in Node::forward_to,
  rewrite, forward_or_copy, and rewrite_from_partial that the node written
  is not a literal node (gc_is_literal).  A literal
  outside the tables is a node of its module: the generator
  (internLiteralNode; symbol kind LITERAL_NODE, code L) defines
  static Node * const CyL... = literal_node(info, value) once per spelling
  in the .strings section, and the module makes the node when it loads.  A
  literal inside the tables stays int_(1) or char_(U'a'), which the C++
  compiler folds to a load from the table.  A literal that is the result of
  a step is still written into the redex.  FORMAT_VERSION 5 -> 6.  Why one
  node per value is safe: a node of a primitive value is never written (a
  rewrite targets a redex and a forward a redex or a free variable;
  forward_or_copy copies from the shared node into the redex), the bindings
  compare primitive values by value (add_binding reads the value word
  through obj_id), the repr form marks only a cycle with an ellipsis, and
  the Python binding Node.forward_to now refuses a primitive value.  New in
  the bindings: SMALL_INT_MIN, SMALL_INT_MAX, SMALL_CHAR_MAX, small_int,
  small_char, gc_literal_count, gc_is_literal.  Found on the way: ICurry
  spells a negative
  literal as negate applied to a positive one, so generated code never
  spells one.  Measured with the harness (C++ backend, three repetitions,
