- Weak free-variable table and finalizers (G3).  The free-variable table
  (RuntimeState::vtable) was a root of the collector and never shrank, so
  every free variable and its generator stayed alive for the life of the
  runtime state, and a lookup by id from another runtime state (a nested
  evaluation started from a Python callback, or a value with a free variable
  evaluated again) found nothing and crashed (status 139).  Done: the table
  lives in the InterpreterState, the owner of the ids, which registers
  itself with the collector; and it is weak: the mark phase pushes no entry
  of it, and after the mark an entry whose node is unmarked is dropped
  (sweep_freevar_tables in gc/wdgc.cpp).  A live configuration keeps the
  variables it names by id, pushed with the queue that holds it
  (push_queue_roots): its residuals (ready asks for them), the keys of its
  bindings (fork applies a binding through the generator of the variable),
  and the ids in its strict constraints (UnionFind::united records every id
  passed to unite; the root of a group stands for the group and receives the
  generator of a narrowed member at the fork).  Every other lookup starts
  from a node in the graph.  A direct-mapped cache of the ids pushed in the
  collection keeps the cost of the binding keys low (the configurations of
  one queue name the same few variables).  get_freevar returns nullptr for a
  missing entry, get_generator throws std::logic_error, and fork and ready
  tolerate it.  The copiers share a free variable instead of copying it
  (copy_node and GraphCopier in graph/copy.cpp), so one node stands for one
  id: a copy with the same id narrowed apart from the variable, and a value
  handed to Python held a node the runtime could not find; now (x, x) with x
  free evaluates to 2 and 4 in a new runtime state.  Finalizers: a generator
  node (a Python iterator as a Curry list) owns one reference to its
  iterator; Node.create takes it (the Arg only borrows the object, the FIXME
  leak is gone), a copy of the node takes one of its own, the step hands it
  to the node of the rest or releases it when the iterator is exhausted,
  and the sweep releases it for a node that dies unstepped, after the sweep
  (release_pending), because a release may run Python code.  Audit of the
  other foreign slots: a _biString node points into the static strings of a
  module, or into the one message buffer of intern_message; the queue and
  the set of a SetEval node go through the registries; nothing else owns a
  resource.  sprite-exec --stats and curry.stats gain gc_seconds, the
  seconds spent in collections, after compile.  Measured on the C++
  backend: a program that narrows a million variables in sequence
  (CxxFreeVars.narrowMany) runs under a 1 GiB cap with 107 MB peak RSS, 45
  collections, and leaves no node and no table entry behind, the same as a
  run of a hundred thousand; Last takes 0.59 s of CPU against 0.73 s (0.80)
  with a peak RSS of 104 MB against 133 MB (0.78); the other nine programs
  of the measured set (QueensSet9, QueensSet, PermSort, Tak1, Fib,
  Queens10, Primes, ColormapFree, PaliFunPats) are within the noise band in
  CPU and peak RSS, with equal counters.  QueensSet9's collector time did
  not move (1.45 s before, 1.47 s after, median of three): that program
  makes 43 free variables in all, so the table was not what its collections
  marked; its live set is the configurations of the lazy set functions
  consumed only in part (120335 configurations in 10212 queues at the
  largest collection, 391384 nodes marked, 12.5 percent of the heap;
  mark 0.165 s, sweep 0.034 s, registries 0.085 s, GC_REPORT build), and
  the cost per collection is the roots of those configurations and the
  destruction of the dead queues.  Tests: unit_cxx_freevars.py (a million
  narrowings under a 1 GiB cap, the table between collections, the shared
  variable of a value and its evaluation, stress mode against plain mode on
  narrowing, binding, unification, a group whose root left the expression,
  a configuration that wakes on a variable another one narrowed, and a
  functional pattern; the finalizer of the generator nodes), unit_cxx_gc.py
  and unit_api.py (gc_seconds).  Left: a free variable built from Python
  (curry.free(), Node.create of _Free) is not in the table and has id 0
  unless given one, so curry.free() inside a goal still gives wrong values;
  intern_message keeps one message, so a second error overwrites the text
  of an earlier _biString node; the Python backend keeps its strong table;
  the dense vector of UnionFind is copied whole on the first unite of a
  configuration, which makes a loop that unites two fresh variables per
  step quadratic (uniteMany); the __deepcopy__ binding takes no memo
  argument.  QueensSet9 needs cheaper configurations, not a smaller table:
  a flat map for the bindings, pooled parts of a configuration, and a
  cheaper destruction of a dead queue (G1, G4).
- Block heap for the C++ runtime (G4, first milestone: the heap with the
  existing mark phase; the conservative C stack scan is not done).  Every
  node came from malloc or a free list through an out-of-line node_reserve
  that pushed a 16-byte entry onto a global vector, and the sweep walked
  that vector and touched every node for its mark bit in the info pointer.
  Done: gc/blockheap.cpp.  Chunks of 1 MB from aligned_alloc are cut into
  64 KB blocks; a block holds slots of one size class (16 to 512 bytes in
  steps of 8) after a header with two side bitmaps, alloc (the slots that
  hold a node) and mark; the block of a node is its address masked, the
  slot a reciprocal multiplication; a larger node gets a span of its own
  with the same header.  node_reserve is an inline fast path in
  graph/memory.hpp (the run of free slots of the size class: a load, a
  compare, an add, a store; the run array is a global of the runtime,
  which a generated module reaches through one indirection), node_refill
  finds the next run of zero alloc bits in the current block (a lazy
  sweep), or takes a block from the class list, the pool of empty blocks,
  or a new chunk; node_commit is inline true.  Counts of nodes and
  allocations are kept per run.  The sweep visits blocks, not nodes: the
  population count of the marks gives the survivors, alloc takes the marks,
  an empty block goes to the pool, a dead span back to the system.  The
  generator nodes are registered at creation (gc_register_generator), so
  the finalizer sweep walks that list.  Nested evaluations: every block
  records the time it left the pool (seq); gc_enter_evaluation records the
  counter in O(1); at a nested collection every allocated slot of an older
  block is a root, the block is not swept and not listed until the
  outermost collection (a superset of the old floor rule, bounded by the
  free room of the heap at the enter).  One ThreadHeap holds the runs, the
  class lists, the pool, and the block list; a worker thread of the
  parallel prototype gets one of its own.  The verifier (verify_heap) runs
  after the mark phase in stress mode and on request (gc_verify in the
  bindings): every marked node is a slot start of a known block (a sorted
  table of the chunks and spans), its alloc bit is set, its info table is
  sane, its alloc_size fits the slot, and every successor is null, a
  literal, the static object of a pinned info table, or a marked slot
  start; a request checks the bitmaps of every block as well.  The
  instrumented build (COUNTERS=1) keeps the creator of a node in a table
  beside its block, written by node_refill: that runtime never publishes a
  run, so every allocation goes through it, and generated code is the same
  in both builds (node_creator is a function now; literal nodes have no
  prefix word any more).  The pushed-id cache of the free-variable roots is
  stamped per collection instead of cleared (an 8 KB fill per collection
  showed in stress mode), and retire_runs visits the classes with a block
  only.  Bindings: gc_verify, gc_block_count, gc_heap_bytes.  Measured
  with the harness (C++ backend, three repetitions, CPU seconds, before to
  after, ratio; steps and forks equal): Tak1 1.71 to 1.29 (0.75), Fib 0.59
  to 0.34 (0.58), Reverse 0.66 to 0.58 (0.88), Queens10 2.91 to 1.77
  (0.61), PermSort 7.58 to 5.20 (0.69), QueensSet 1.05 to 0.76 (0.73),
  QueensSet9 7.34 to 5.15 (0.70), Primes 3.30 to 2.24 (0.68).  Peak RSS
  (memory suite, collector on / off, in MiB as compare prints it): Tak1 105
  to 65 / 1710 to 962, Fib 195 to 115 / 232 to 131, Reverse 83 to 60 / 547
  to 232, PermSort 466 to 312 / 1710 to 958, QueensSet 139 to 105 / 325 to
  231, QueensSet9 395 to 321 / 1758 to 1228.  Nodes per step unchanged (Tak1
  0.9333, Queens10
  1.0437), values equal.  perf stat on Tak1: 16.18 G instructions at 3.28
  per cycle, 8.8 M cache misses (17.87 G, 2.71, 15.4 M after T6).  Stress
  mode: a collection on a heap of 5 live nodes costs 0.42 us (0.07 to 0.12
  before), on 1704 live nodes 25 us, most of it the verifier; unit_cxx_gc
  in stress mode takes 43 s (29 before), unit_cxx_freevars 68 s (37).
  Tests: unit_cxx_heap.py with data/curry/CxxHeap.curry (a constructor of
  70 fields, so its node is a span) and data/cxx/heap_test.cpp (consecutive
  slots of one run, exact counts, the lazy sweep reusing the freed slots in
  address order, a bounded heap over cycles of short-lived nodes, a span
  kept and given back, the rule for nested evaluations through
  EvaluationScope, the generator finalizer with the hooks registered, and
  the verifier finding a successor in a freed slot); the exact counts, the
  bounded heap of a walk, spans through collections, the verifier after
  walks, set functions, and a callback evaluation and in stress mode, the
  refusal inside an evaluation, and the step of tak calling node_refill and
  neither node_reserve nor node_commit (objdump).  unit_cxx_gc.py: the
  collector-off child of the 1 GiB test walks three million cells (a
  million fit under the cap in the dense heap); unit_cxx_counters.py: the
  expected fields include gc_seconds (the instrumented build was not run
  since G3 added it).  Docs: the environment variables, tests/README, the
  gc README.  Left: the conservative C stack scan (stage B of the memory
  note), so a collection in a Python callback still treats every older
  node as a root, and in a coarser way than before (the free slots of an
  older block filled after the enter are roots too); the W7 clause of the
  memory note, QueensSet9 with the collector on not slower than off, is
  open: the entry above compares collector on before and after, and on
  against off the collector costs 1.51 times the CPU (5.23 s against 3.46
  s, memory suite, record phase2-2026-10-04) with 1.29 s in collections,
  25 percent of the run, above the 15 percent trigger of W9 (cheaper
  configurations, G1 and G4 follow-up); memory is never
  given back to the system except the spans of large nodes (a chunk whose
  16 blocks are all empty could be freed); the heap is one global object
  reached by a global run array, not yet per thread; the stress mode is
  slower by the verifier; blocks are 64 KB and the largest small slot 512
  bytes, neither measured against alternatives.
- MPS spike for Gate G (G5).  The 2023 draft of a Memory Pool System back
  end (gc/mps.cpp) did not build, reused the runtime's Fwd info as the
  collector's forwarding marker (unsound), had no valid cold end for its
  stack root, no roots beyond the queues, and generations of 150 KB.  Done:
  make GC=mps builds the runtime with the Memory Pool System of the
  submodule extern/mps (release 1.118, one C object, the hot variety) in
  place of gc/blockheap.cpp and gc/wdgc.cpp; the default stays wdgc.  The
  choice reaches the installation as sysconfig/cxx_gc, the toolchain
  compiles generated modules with -DSPRITE_GC_MPS, and the ABI stamp
  includes the collector, so a switch recompiles every module once (touch
  Make.config, as for COUNTERS; the sysconfig values depend on Make.config
  now).  The back end: one AMC pool of two generations (8 MB and 64 MB by
  default, SPRITE_GC_MPS_NURSERY_KB and SPRITE_GC_MPS_GEN1_KB), an object
  format that reads the size of a node from its info table, forwarding and
  padding objects of the collector's own (GcFwd, GcFwdSz, GcPad, GcPadSz),
  and five roots: an exact root over every registered queue (the root, the
  bindings, and the error of each configuration), an ambiguous root over
  the scan cursors (interior pointers pin the spine), an ambiguous root
  over the nodes Python holds (they never move), an exact root over the
  free-variable tables, and the thread root with the top of the stack from
  pthread_getattr_np as the cold end (the frames of a suspended step and of
  an enclosing evaluation are found there, so a nested collection needs no
  rule about older nodes).  The in-place writers of node.hxx (rewrite,
  forward_or_copy) and rewrite_from_partial pad the slack of a larger block
  with a pad object, because the format walks a segment object by object.
  node_refill is the only allocation path (the runs stay empty); it commits
  the object before the caller writes it and writes a pad object into it
  first, so the heap is consistent at every moment (create_flat allocated
  its fresh variables inside the window and makes them first now).  The
  address-keyed algorithms (the graph copier, the equality, show, the
  unique visitor) hold a GcClamp, which clamps the arena.  MPS schedules
  its collections itself, incrementally, behind protection barriers; the
  safepoint of the scheduler pumps its messages (finalization of generator
  and SetEval nodes, which every creator registers now:
  gc_register_seteval; a queue is destroyed when the last SetEval node that
  refers to it has died) and runs a full collection in stress mode, from
  Python, at SPRITE_GC_THRESHOLD allocations when set, and at the
  configuration threshold.  A SIGSEGV handler installed on top of the MPS
  handler counts the barrier faults and the time in them (gc_fault_count,
  gc_backend_stats, gc_backend in the bindings).  Found on the way: MPS
  scans every root at the flip and admits the ambiguous and the exact rank
  there, so a root of the weak rank fails an assertion; the free-variable
  table is a strong root under MPS, as before G3.  The sets of set
  functions are not freed under MPS.  Measured with the harness (C++
  backend, three repetitions, CPU seconds, the block heap of G4 to MPS,
  ratio; steps and forks equal; each program in a fresh process): Tak1 1.30
  to 2.85 (2.19), Queens10 1.74 to 2.72 (1.57), PermSort 5.13 to 14.21
  (2.77), QueensSet9 5.10 to 19.41 (3.80), Primes 2.27 to 4.16 (1.83).
  Peak RSS with the collector on: Tak1 65 to 44 MB, Queens10 60 to 69,
  PermSort 312 to 830, QueensSet9 321 to 538, Primes 62 to 116.  The
  collector's own statistics over one evaluation through the API, each in
  a fresh process: Tak1 112 collections, 10 barrier faults, 0.91 s in the
  allocation slow path (where MPS traces); Queens10 171 collections, 11
  faults, 0.73 s; PermSort 106 collections, 8995 faults (0.07 s in the
  handler), 9.6 s in the slow path, 722 MB committed, 427 MB of it spare;
  QueensSet9 67 collections and one full one, 11810 faults (0.11 s), 9.0 s
  in the slow path, 52413 clamps; Primes 212 collections, no fault, 1.6 s.
  perf on Tak1: 44 percent of the samples in the kernel (the protection and
  page traffic of the arena), 37 percent in the runtime library (MPS
  included), 14 percent in the module; the nursery size moves nothing
  (Tak1 2.85, 2.87, 2.83 s with 8, 32, 64 MB; Queens10 2.72, 3.20, 3.77).
  A probe that ran the five programs in one process put Primes at 55 s:
  the dead data of the earlier programs in the older generations is
  rescanned by every nursery collection until a full collection comes.
  Signals: with Python's faulthandler enabled at startup the MPS handler is
  installed after it (at the first allocation) and takes the faults;
  nothing is printed.  faulthandler.enable() after the first allocation
  puts Python's handler in front; the test child of that case took no
  fault, so the case is not exercised.  Near an address-space cap MPS
  collects instead of failing: the out-of-memory child of
  unit_cxx_runtime.py reaches 905 MB of RSS under a 1 GiB cap and does not
  end in 60 s, where the block heap raises MemoryError at once.  Decision
  (Gate G): MPS is not adopted.  The rule asked for 20 percent less CPU on
  Tak1 and Queens10 and no loss on PermSort and QueensSet9; MPS costs 1.6
  to 3.8 times the CPU on all five and 1.7 to 2.7 times the memory on the
  search programs (QueensSet9 1.67, PermSort 2.66).  The block heap stays
  the collector; the MPS back
  end stays in the tree behind make GC=mps as the record of the spike.
  Tests: unit_cxx_mps.py (the selection of the collector and the toolchain
  flags on every build; under MPS: the values of programs that collect many
  times, a node Python holds keeps its address, an explicit collection
  reclaims a dropped value, the finalizer of the generator nodes, the queue
  of a set function freed, a nested evaluation, the threshold, the fault
  counter and the statistics, the stress mode, faulthandler at startup and
  after the first allocation); the tests of the block heap, of its
  threshold policy, of the exact counts of queues and sets, of the weak
  free-variable table, and of the out-of-memory child skip under MPS
  (cytest.skipUnlessGcBackend).  The unit suite passes on both builds.
  Left: the inline allocation fast path of memory.hpp is not wired to the
  MPS allocation point (every allocation is a call); the weak table, the
  freeing of sets, and the scheduler counters are not supported under MPS;
  gc_verify reports nothing there; the enable-after-allocation case of
  faulthandler needs a child that provokes a barrier fault.
- Process-split experiment for Gate Q1 (Q1, issue #29).  The
  parallel-evaluation gate asks whether independent parts of a search, run
  on separate workers, could pay: the speedup bound of a split by hand, and
  the work the parts duplicate.  Done, with no runtime change: three
  modules under tests/data/curry/benchmarks/split spell SearchQueens,
  PermSort, and QueensSet whole and split into 2, 4, and 8 parts (the goals
  main and part2_0 .. part8_7).  PermSortSplit fixes the first one, two, or
  three binary choices of the search as the program makes them (the
  insertions of 3, 4, and 5 into the growing permutation; insert forks
  first at the back of the input): it spells the configuration after those
  forks by hand and inserts the other elements on top as perm does.
  SearchQueensSplit fixes the position of the first element: u is a list
  of i free variables in place of the narrowed free list (the branch (_:_)
  i times, then []); the eight positions go to the parts by position modulo
  the count, joined by ?.  QueensSetSplit fixes the first element of the
  permutation (the branch of ndinsert that puts j in front after 1 .. j-1
  went behind: j : perm of the rest); the eight first elements go to the
  parts likewise.  The parts of a split partition the permutations.  Every
  function takes the size, so unit_split.py checks the partition at size 5
  (120 permutations) and the solutions at size 6 (5 on the Python backend)
  against the original modules.  The whole of each split module takes the
  steps of the original (SearchQueensSplit 41373250, QueensSetSplit
  2152979, PermSortSplit 12531155 against 12531154).  The harness got a
  split suite (-s split: the whole as in the throughput suite, and every
  part as sprite-exec -t --stats -m PROGRAMSplit -g partK_I in a process of
  its own; the variants are whole and K/I; three repetitions by default)
  and a split command (run_benchmarks split FILE: per program and part
  count the speedup bound, the whole over the longest part, and the
  duplicated work, the sum of the parts over the whole, by CPU seconds and
  by steps and forks; --parts lists the parts; -m picks wall or eval_wall);
  the counters command names an item with a variant program:variant.
  Measured on the C++ backend (plain build, three repetitions, CPU seconds
  of the whole process, which includes about 0.16 s for the start of the
  process; steps exact; the machine had a load of 1.6 to 4 from another
  process).  SearchQueens, whole 4.59 s, 41.37 M steps, 1.08 M forks: two
  parts 3.25 and 3.02 s, bound 1.41 (by steps 1.62), duplicated work 1.22
  by steps, 1.37 by CPU, 1.26 by forks; four parts 1.80, 1.60, 1.45, and
  1.36 s, bound 2.55 (steps 3.15), duplication 1.22 by steps; eight parts
  0.69 to 0.83 s and one disturbed sample of 1.26 s, bound 3.64 (steps
  5.94, eval_wall 4.81), duplication 1.22 by steps.  The 22 percent are
  the same at every count: a part evaluates permute xs under its own u,
  which the whole shares between the alternatives of the narrowing.
  PermSort, whole 4.71 s, 12.53 M steps, 1.59 M forks: two parts 2.68 and
  2.05 s (6.96 and 5.57 M steps), bound 1.76 (steps 1.80), duplication 1.00
  by steps and by forks; four parts 5.11, 1.85, 3.71, and 1.85 M steps,
  bound 2.41 (steps 2.45), duplication 1.00; eight parts 4.49, 0.62, 1.24,
  0.62, 3.10, 0.62, 1.24, and 0.62 M steps, bound 2.61 (steps 2.79),
  duplication 1.00: nothing is duplicated, but the static split is
  unbalanced, because the sub-tree in which 1 stays in front, the side of
  the solution, holds most of the work.  QueensSet, whole 0.80 s, 2.15 M
  steps, 0.51 M forks: two parts 1.080 and 1.078 M steps, bound 1.77
  (steps 1.99, eval_wall 2.34); four parts 0.51 to 0.57 M steps, bound 2.12
  (steps 3.81, eval_wall 4.28); eight parts 0.24 to 0.30 M steps, bound
  2.98 (steps 7.20, eval_wall 9.56); duplication 1.00 by steps at every
  count.  The CPU bound of QueensSet is the start of the process: a part
  of eight evaluates in 0.05 to 0.06 s.  The cost per step falls in a part
  (a smaller heap, fewer collections): the evaluation times of the parts
  sum to 0.84 to 0.97 of the whole's for PermSort and 0.74 to 0.85 for
  QueensSet.  The scheduler counters of the 30 programs on this tree (make
  COUNTERS=1, one repetition; the records are in the measure directory of
  the build tree): the 16 deterministic programs have a serial fraction of
  1.000 and one configuration; the 12 search programs have 0.000 (RegExp
  0.065, Half 0.002), a queue of up to 94135 configurations (PermSortPeano;
  PermSort 94096, SearchQueens 85637, ColormapFree 27638, ColormapChoice
  15208; Last 6, RegExp 8), median lifetimes of 0 to 8 steps (means 2.0 to
  32.5), shared fractions from 0.163 (PermSortPeano) and 0.194
  (SearchQueens) to 1.000 (ColormapChoice, ColormapFree), and failed
  fractions from 0.002 (Half) to 0.654 (PermSortPeano); QueensSet runs
  0.966 of its steps inside set functions.  PokerChoice and PokerFree do
  not compile (currypp), as before.  The parts carry the fractions of
  their whole (PermSort: 0.492 shared in every part): the shared counter
  measures the sharing between the configurations inside a part, which
  the split does not duplicate; what a split duplicates is the work above
  its point.  The counters build costs 0.94 to 2.11 times the CPU of the
  plain build on the 45 split items (40 slower, 5 the same; every
  allocation goes through node_refill there), with equal steps and forks
  on every item.  Decision (Gate Q1): go.  The rule asked for five
  programs with a serial fraction below 0.5, or a split bound above 2 at
  four parts for two programs; twelve programs have a serial fraction
  below 0.07, and the bound at four parts is 2.55, 2.41, and 2.12 by CPU
  (3.15, 2.45, and 3.81 by steps) for the three programs, with 0 to 22
  percent duplicated work.  The go rests on the split bound, by steps
  first: the CPU of a part includes the start of its process.  The clause
  of the parallel-evaluation note that asks for a median configuration
  lifetime that supports batching is not met (the medians are 0 to 8
  steps), so Q2 and Q3 are judged by a design in which a worker owns a
  subtree, with a dynamic split.  E6 continues with the independent-world
  design (Q2, Q3), on the terms the numbers set: a worker must own a
  subtree (a configuration lives a handful of steps, so a hand-over per
  configuration cannot pay); the split must be dynamic, or deeper than the
  first choices (the static eight parts of PermSort range from 0.62 M to
  4.49 M steps); and the bounds assume no cost for the start of a worker,
  the hand-over, or the merge of the values.  Tests: unit_split.py (the
  partition of the permutations and of the solutions at a small size on
  both backends; the goals), unit_benchmarks.py (the split programs and
  variants, the items and their commands, the default repeat, the variant
  in the counters table, the split command on hand-made records with a
  part missing, a part failed, and the whole failed, and the smoke test of
  QueensSet whole and in two parts on the C++ backend).  Docs: tests/README
  (section 9), the benchmark README.  Left: the split is static and by
  hand, for three programs; the parts run one after the other, so the
  bound says nothing about the memory bandwidth of four processes at once;
  the CPU of a part includes the start of its process (use the bound by
  steps, or -m eval_wall, for a short program); the sample of SearchQueens
  8/6 was disturbed by another process (its evaluation 0.91 s against 0.51
  to 0.67 s of the other parts; the bound by steps does not depend on it);
  the counters of the M4 entry were taken on the pre-Phase-2 tree, and the
  shared fractions moved a little with the in-place rewrites (PermSortPeano
  0.10 to 0.163, ColormapChoice 0.99 to 1.000).
- Nightly performance job with history (M5, issue E1).  The harness had no
  history: the baseline files under tests/data/curry/benchmarks/results
  were the only records, and nothing ran the suites on a schedule.  Done:
  the workflow .github/workflows/perf.yml runs on a schedule (05:17 UTC)
  and on request, builds Sprite on an ubuntu-24.04 runner without ccache,
  and runs three suites on the C++ backend through
  .github/scripts/run-perf.sh: the throughput suite on a fixed set of ten
  programs with three repetitions (NIGHTLY in lib/benchmarks/__init__.py:
  Tak1, Fib, Reverse, Primes, Queens10, PermSort, SearchQueens, QueensSet,
  QueensSet9, Last; the new --nightly option of run_benchmarks selects the
  set, with the expression item in the compile suite and every item of
  the import suite), the compile suite on the same programs (three
  repetitions), and the import suite (five).  The records go to the branch
  perf-history of the repository, written with the token of the workflow
  (permissions: contents: write; no secret) by
  .github/scripts/perf-history.sh, which fetches the branch into a
  worktree (or starts it with an orphan worktree), runs the new history
  command of the harness, commits, and pushes, three attempts when the
  branch moved.  run_benchmarks history DIR FILE... appends the records to
  records/SUITE.jsonl (a record file like any other, so compare reads it),
  writes points.json (one compact object per record, derived from the
  record files) and a README, and prints each new record against the
  previous record of its item with the rows and the summary of the compare
  command; the script puts that table in the step summary.  The job fails
  on a failed run only, after the records are kept as an artifact and the
  history is written; a slow run is never a failure.  The page
  docs/source/extra/perf/index.html (perf/index.html of the built
  documentation, through html_extra_path; linked from the new Performance
  page) is plain HTML and JavaScript without a library: one figure per
  suite made of small panels, one per item (ten or more series in one plot
  would exceed the eight hues a categorical palette allows), each a single
  line of the chosen metric over time with the last value at its end, a
  hollow marker where the rewrite steps changed, a cross on the baseline
  where a run failed, a crosshair with a readout (value, date, commit,
  counters, status) on hover and on the arrow keys, a delta against the
  previous run beyond the 10 percent band, and a table view; the metric
  and the range are selectable.  On GitHub Pages the page reads
  points.json from the perf-history branch through
  raw.githubusercontent.com; elsewhere from a file beside itself, or from
  the URL of ?history=.  Measured here (the commands of the job, the C++
  backend, two runs one after the other on this tree, CPU seconds, the
  counters equal on every item): throughput Tak1 1.297 -> 1.270, Fib 0.353
  -> 0.339, Reverse 0.589 -> 0.581, Primes 2.190 -> 2.229, Queens10 1.740
  -> 1.747, PermSort 5.067 -> 4.930, SearchQueens 4.776 -> 4.904, QueensSet
  0.777 -> 0.770, QueensSet9 5.121 -> 5.086, Last 0.441 -> 0.420 (10 same);
  the compile suite 22 items same (cold compiles 0.92 to 1.51 s, warm 0.48
  to 1.01 s, the expression 0.80 s cold and 0.39 s warm); the import suite
  4 same (python 0.025, import 0.10, prelude 0.16, hello 0.16 s).  The
  three suites take 1m30, 1m11, and 3 s here.  Published twice through the
  script to a bare repository made for the purpose: the first run started
  the branch, the second appended 36 records and printed the ratios; the
  page rendered the result under node (3 figures, 36 panels, 36 lines).
  Tests: unit_benchmarks.py (the nightly set and --nightly, the summary of
