- Verification velocity: the test runner (V1, V2), deterministic benchmark
  columns (M6), the parallel build and ccache (V3), and the developer setup
  (W1).  Development is limited by verification wall time: every test file
  ran in its own process, one at a time, under ulimit -v 6 GB, about 8.5
  min for the unit files on the C++ backend and 9 min on the Python
  backend; builds never passed -j; benchmarks reported load-dependent
  seconds.  Done:
  (1) Test runner, tests/lib/testrunner (python -m testrunner; run_tests
  execs it): one process per file and backend in a session of its own, a
  watchdog that walks /proc every half second, sums the resident sets of
  the file's session and of the descendants of its process (parent links,
  so a child that started a session of its own counts; the members of one
  poll are the roots of the next), records the peak, and kills the session
  over its cap or past --timeout; admission under -j and a memory budget
  (--mem, 60 percent of MemAvailable by default) with backfill, the two
  backends of one file never together, EXCLUSIVE files alone
  (unit_currylib.py); tests/manifest.json (format 1: duration, peak,
  counts, machine size per file and backend; the cap is twice the peak, at
  least 512 MB, 2 GB without a peak; a file without a duration is assumed
  to take the median of the measured ones); --update-manifest (the peak
  only grows, the duration only from a run with a unittest verdict);
  --fast, --changed REV with RULES and a fallback to everything for the
  unknown, --list, --prepare (sprite-make over the shared corpus, one
  directory and process at a time, before the parallel files, because the
  front end, the ICurry and JSON writers, the code generators, and the C++
  compiler write their products in place); SIGINT, SIGTERM, and SIGHUP set
  a flag that the run loop turns into the kill of every session and status
  130; a pattern that matches no file is status 2; -v at width 1 streams
  the output and hands the file the standard input (the editor targets of
  tests/Makefile pass -v).  The committed manifest holds four durations and
  no peaks; DEFAULT_JOBS is 1 until the calibration below.  Tests:
  unit_runner.py (54 tests on small Python children: admission, watchdog,
  timeout, a child in its own session, an orphan, interrupts, manifest,
  rules, report, cli, prepare).  Known: the sum counts a shared page once
  per process (Pss from smaps_rollup costs 14 ms per read against 0.016 ms
  for stat, measured on a 600 MB process, so the runner keeps stat); a
  spike shorter than a poll in a child can be missed; a process forked
  between the scan and the kill by a parent that is already dead escapes.
  (2) Deterministic columns: the harness counts the user-space
  instructions of the child and its descendants with perf stat
  (-e instructions:u) in one repetition after the measured ones, so the
  measured seconds stay free of perf (the repetition is the last sample,
  "perf": true; perf is detected at start; --no-perf skips it); meta holds
  cpu_model, cores, mem_gb and no host name; schema 2, schema 1 loads and
  compares; compare prints the machine of each file, warns when they
  differ, shows the ratio of the instructions, and has --deterministic:
  the instructions decide with a tolerance of 1 percent, steps, forks, and
  collections are listed beyond it, wall and CPU seconds are advisory
  (~cpu, ~wall); --strict now fails on an item without the metric, so a
  deterministic gate on records without instructions fails instead of
  passing with no comparison.  Measured on a loaded machine: two runs of
  Hello differed by 14 percent in CPU seconds and by 0.02 percent in
  instructions; perf adds about 20 ms of CPU per run.  Left: an allocation
  counter from the collector as a further column (needs a runtime change);
  the nightly job records the new columns once perf is on the runner; the
  baseline of 2026-10-03 has no instructions, so a comparison by
  instructions needs a new baseline; the hybrid-processor sum is untested
  on such a machine.
  (3) Parallel build and ccache: configure --jobs N|auto writes JOBS into
  Make.config and the root Makefile turns it into -j (auto is nproc at
  make time; a -j on the command line wins, and so does make JOBS=N);
  every recipe of the root Makefile says $(MAKE), so the job server
  reaches the sub-makes; the order rules python | cyrt, shlibs:: | libs,
  shlib_extra | libcyrt.a, and .NOTPARALLEL in curry/Makefile keep make -j
  safe; configure --with-ccache puts ccache in front of CC and CXX and
  makes tools/cxx a script that runs ccache in front of the post-install
  compiler.  Checked with dry runs, make -p, scratch models, and
  unit_configure.py only; no real parallel build has run; ccache was not
  installed.  DEFAULT_JOBS in configure is '1' until the trial below.
  Known: every make stage re-archives and relinks libcyrt.so because the
  archive members carry full paths (ar -P); harmless.  Left: the
  post-install compiles do not hit the cache until Cpp2So compiles with -c
  and links separately; PrecompiledHeader.flavor should then read the
  compiler behind the wrapper; CI keeps its own wrapper script until the
  option is proven.
  (4) Developer setup: scripts/setup-dev-machine.sh --prefix DIR (apt
  check without sudo, PAKCS with its SHA-256, optional icurry, the
  checkout with install and object-root linked into the prefix, the conda
  environment from conda/dev-environment.yml, configure, make stage, a
  smoke test on both backends, the next steps), docs/source/
  DeveloperSetup.rst, tests/unit_setup_script.py (dry runs with stand-in
  tools).  Review fixes: --jobs auto goes to configure as auto;
  CCACHE_DIR=PREFIX/ccache is part of the clean environment; configure is
  skipped while Make.config exists and PREFIX/configure-args holds the
  same flags, because configure empties install and object-root
  (--reconfigure forces it).  Verified in dry runs and the unit test only;
  the first real run on the new machine must verify the PAKCS build, the
  conda solve, the --icurry step, make stage, and the smoke test, then the
  personal rules file gets the paths of that machine.
  The quiet window (one agent, nothing else on the machine), in order:
    1. Calibrate the manifest: from tests/, in the clean environment,
       ./run_tests -j 1 --backend both --update-manifest (about an hour);
       review git diff tests/manifest.json; run the files reported as
       "killed: memory" again with --update-manifest (their caps follow the
       recorded peaks); commit the manifest.  Then a parallel trial:
       ./run_tests -j auto --backend both --prepare; compare the wall time
       and the failures with the serial run.
    2. The make -j trial: install ccache; re-run configure with the current
       options plus --jobs auto --with-ccache (configure empties install
       and object-root); time make stage from clean with JOBS=1, then with
       JOBS=auto; then a no-op make stage; then a rebuild after a touch of
       src/cyrt/graph/node.hpp and of src/cyrt/state/rts.cpp (libcyrt.so
       must relink); run unit_cxx_toolchain.py, unit_prebuild.py, and
       unit_currylib.py.
    3. make -j12 stage from clean three times; diff -r two of the installed
       trees (minus time stamps); a race shows as a difference or a
       failure.
    4. The ccache trial: ccache -z; make stage from clean; ccache -s; again
       from clean; the runtime objects must hit on the second build.
    5. Flip the defaults: DEFAULT_JOBS = 'auto' in tests/lib/testrunner/
       __init__.py (after step 1) and DEFAULT_JOBS = 'auto' in configure
       (after steps 2 to 4); update the sentences that say the default is
       1 in tests/README section 10, AGENTS.md, and docs/source/
       Installation/ConfiguringSprite.rst; run unit_runner.py and
       unit_configure.py (they read the constants); let CI replace its
       sprite-cxx wrapper with --with-ccache.
    6. Merge checks: merge py314 into the branch; make stage in the clean
       environment; ./run_tests -j auto --backend both --prepare; a
       benchmark smoke run against a fresh baseline with
       ./run_benchmarks compare --deterministic; then merge.

2026-10-04: the C++ compile-time series (examples 20 to 23)
- Four examples model the compile-time algorithms of a C++ compiler over
  one algebraic model of C++ types, the shared modules under examples/cxx:
  template argument deduction and the partial ordering of partial
  specializations (20), overload resolution (21), a bounded check of the
  traits against the standard (22) and member layout with a search for
  the order of the smallest size (23).  Template parameters are free
  variables, search is non-determinism, and set functions collect the
  results.  The motivation is a future integration with the Circle C++
  compiler, whose compile-time metaprogramming can run Python; Sprite
  would supply the search and the unification, Circle the types.  The
  model simplifies C++: no enumerations, unions, wide character types,
  noexcept or ref-qualified functions, non-type template parameters,
  user-defined conversions, templates in overload resolution,
  argument-dependent lookup, member functions, default arguments, class
  layout (a class has no size), base classes, bit-fields, alignas or
  packing; deduction has no function-pointer or derived-to-base
  conversions (the qualification conversion and forwarding references
  were added to deduceCall when example 20 grew to 33 rows); reference
  parameters are references to const, and to functions, only; common_type
  covers the arithmetic types; the data model is LP64.  Each README lists
  what its example leaves out.
- Review of the series, applied: common_type<T, T> is decay_t<T>, not the
  promoted type, and common_type decays its operands; deduce with the
  pattern T const& takes the const off the argument too, so int const[3]
  deduces T = int[3], and the rules of deduceRef no longer overlap;
  std::nullptr_t does not convert to bool in a call, and the tie-breaker
  of [over.ics.rank]/4.1 names pointers and pointers to members only; a
  reference to const of array type, and a reference to a function, bind
  an argument of that type directly, with no decay; sizeof of an array of
  unknown bound has no value; the printer puts a space before a name or a
  member-pointer declarator ("Foo* Foo::*", "char const* payload"); the
  constructors of Choice are Chosen, Tied and NoMatch, so that a module
  may import CxxType and CxxOverload together; the well-formed generator
  of CxxCheck replaced the term generator of CxxType (every type once: 18,
  194, 1245 and 7330 types at depths 0 to 3), which removed the duplicate
  solutions of the inverse questions.
- Generator shape and search cost (C++ backend): the guarded form of the
  generator (build a compound, reject it when a guard on the operand
  fails) made the search t =:= anyType n four times slower at depth 2 and
  25 times slower at depth 3 (33 s instead of 1.4 s over 7330 types) than
  the constructive form (one sub-generator per category, no rejected
  alternative); example 20 on the Python backend went from 39 s to 7 s.  A
  rejected alternative is a live branch of the search until its guard
  fails, and the cost of a search grows faster than the number of its
  branches.  Worth a note in the user guide on the narrowing idiom.
- Defects met by the streams that wrote the examples, not fixed (the
  suspension of (3) on the Python backend was reproduced here): (1, #59) C++
  emitter: a case whose default branch body is a bare literal or a nullary
  constructor (`_ -> False`) is let-lifted by the front end and the
  emitter writes the literal into a cyrt::Variable, which does not
  compile; the shared modules use explicit alternatives or calls in
  default bodies.  (2, #60) Python backend: show of an Int inside a term that
  =:= bound to a free variable does not finish, while arithmetic on it
  works; CxxType.showNat prints the bound of an array by arithmetic.  (3, #61)
  Both backends: a set function applied to a free variable that =:= bound
  under a choice sees the binding of the first alternative in every branch
  (t =:= (Fund Int ? Fund Char) &> sortValues (set1 id t) gives [Fund Int]
  twice); example 21 forces the variable with $## first, which works on
  the C++ backend; on the Python backend $## raises EvaluationSuspended
  and $!! a ValueError inside the backend, so 21 runs on the C++ backend
  only.  (4, #62) Python backend: length of a list of 5040 elements and sortBy
  on 2000 elements raise RecursionError, so a set with more than about
  1000 values cannot be sorted or counted there (example 23 keeps six
  members).  (5, #63) Python backend: a point-free top-level definition (arity
  0) cannot be applied to arguments from Python.  (6) Front end: a where
  clause after a case expression whose alternatives share its indentation
  is not attached to the equation.  (7) Collector stress mode: example 22
  (seven set functions over 1245 types) does not finish in 300 s;
  STRESS_EXCLUDED in unit_examples.py.  (1) and (3) fixed on 2026-10-05
  (the bug batch, below).
- Follow-ups for the series: user-defined conversions and the ranking of
  user-defined conversion sequences; templates in overload resolution
  (deduceCall is the hook) and the preference for a non-template; the
  reference bindings of [over.ics.ref] with value categories; the
  ordering of function templates and non-deduced contexts in deduction; concepts, with constraint subsumption as a
  partial order over conjunctions of atomic constraints, beside the
  partial ordering of specializations; a Struct type with members, so
  that nested aggregates get a layout, with alignas and packing; more
  traits (remove_extent, remove_all_extents, remove_pointer,
  is_bounded_array, ...) and properties over the partial traits with a
  count of outcomes; a unit test file for the shared library (the scratch
  drivers of the streams checked 101, 52 and 65 assertions); a Circle
  driver: a metaprogram that hands the types of the program under
  compilation to the model and asks the questions of the series; and,
  after the typed builder (#56), the inverse questions of examples 20 and
  21 can take a variable made in Python with curry.free() in place of the
  variables their Curry modules declare.

2026-10-04: typed boundary, stage 1 (epic #48)
- Y1, free variables from Python (issue #50, fixes #33).  curry.free()
  built a raw Free node with the id 0 at every occurrence of the marker,
  and no table of the evaluation knew it; curry.choice used the id 0 as
  well.  Done: through curry.expr a free marker is one shared node per
  marker, a call of Prelude.unknown applied to the Data dictionary of Bool
  (unknown never reads it), built at the first occurrence and cached on the
  marker with a weak reference to the interpreter state.  The step of
  unknown creates the variable with a fresh id of the runtime and forwards
  the node, so one marker is one variable in one expression and across
  expressions; curry.reset installs a new state, and the marker becomes a
  new variable there.  A choice marker is a call of Prelude.?, so its id is
  fresh too.  raw_expr keeps the raw Free and Choice nodes with the ids of
  the markers (unit_expr.py unchanged).  RuntimeState.set_goal registers
  the free variables a goal already holds, on both backends (rts.py creates
  vtable before the call; RuntimeState::register_freevars in
  rts_freevars.cpp): an iterative walk with a visited set that follows
  forward nodes and every pointer successor, so a cyclic goal ends.  The
  walk runs behind a counter of the free variables made outside an
  evaluation, InterpreterState.external_freevars on both backends: the
  builder counts markers and raw Free nodes, single_step counts the
  variables of its discarded state (the let xs free of
  compile(mode='expr')), and make_value counts the free variables it copies
  into a value, so a value of one evaluation placed in a later goal is
  registered.  The item of a generator node (a lazy Python iterator) is
  built after the walk, so the step of _biGenerator walks the item on both
  backends.  Measured on the first pass of the item: the C++ walk costs
  about 0.05 us per node (100 ms for a goal of two million nodes that took
  3 s to build from Python), the Python walk about 0.3 us per node; a goal
  without such variables pays nothing.  The bindings expose
  RuntimeState.vtable (read-only) and InterpreterState.external_freevars.
  Tests: unit_free_markers.py (the goal of #33, two markers, one node per
  marker, the reuse of a marker in a second goal with constrEq and with
  the dictionary of =:=, the goal evaluated twice, a cyclic goal, let xs
  free through compile(mode='expr'), the registration, the counter, the
  variables of a single step, a marker inside a lazy iterator, a value with
  a free variable, a marker after reset, raw_expr, and the choice marker
  with fresh ids).  Docs: Quickstart and UsingTheAPI show unknown
  _inst#Prelude.Data#Prelude.Bool before the first step.  Left: the spelling
  curry.eval(getattr(P, '=:='), x, 1) of the issue is a partial application
  until the typed builder (Y7) supplies the Data dictionary; the payload of
  an IO value is not copied on the Python backend and so not counted; the
  typed builder of Y7 passes the Data dictionary of the resolved type to
  unknown.
- Y2, the signature table (issue #51).  The front end writes the type of
  every function and constructor into M.fint, after the dictionary
  translation, and the translation to ICurry dropped it.  Done: the new
  package curry.typecheck (src/python/typecheck/sigtable.py).  An Interface
  indexes one .fint text by byte offset with one regular expression that
  matches a quoted name first, so that a name such as "(,)" or an operator
  never starts or ends an entry, and records the offset of every Func,
  Type, TypeSyn, TypeNew, Cons and NewCons entry; an entry is decoded with
  the FlatCurry reader (toolchain/flat2icurry/flatcurry.py) the first time
  a symbol asks for it, its end found by a bracket scan that skips quoted
  names.  A Scheme holds the quantified variables (the nested quantifier of
  a class method merged in), the context recovered from the leading
  dictionary parameters (() -> _Dict#C t), the type without them, the
  FlatCurry arity, the number of dictionaries, and the FlatCurry type as
  written.  A constructor's scheme comes from its Type or TypeNew entry.
  The printer writes Curry syntax, Num a => a -> a -> a, with the variables
  named by first occurrence, Apply as juxtaposition, and the names of the
  Prelude and of the symbol's own module unqualified (show_scheme takes
  module= and qualify=).  The instance map comes from the _inst#Class#Type
  names of every loaded module, with the instance context from the type;
  the special constructors stay unqualified in the key ([], (), (,), (->));
  a name of another shape is an error that names curry-frontend 2.0.0, and
  the first read of the Prelude interface checks that
  _inst#Prelude.Num#Prelude.Int exists.  One SignatureTable per
  interpreter, interp.sigtable, memoized per module object (weak keys, so
  the modules of compile(mode='expr') are served while they live);
  Interpreter.reset clears it; the parsed files stay in a process-wide
  cache keyed by path, size and mtime (as interfaces.load_interface does),
  purged of vanished files at reset.  The interface file is searched beside
  the module's .icy under the directory of the source and under the module
  root (Y3 writes that copy), then the front end's own copy under
  <root>/.curry/pakcs-3.4.1/ (A/B.fint for A.B) and under the per-directory
  layout.  Sprite's boxed literals Int, Char and Float get the nullary types
  as their schemes; the other symbols of Sprite's own Prelude (IO, ->,
  _biGenerator, _biString) and the lifted _CASE and _LET functions have no
  scheme.  A missing scheme is None through CurryNodeInfo.scheme and
  CurryNodeInfo.signature and an InterfaceError (a CurryTypeError) only
  through SignatureTable.lookup(symbol, required=True): "no type for M.f:
  no FlatCurry interface found; recompile M or use raw_expr".  expr and
  eval never read the table.  Measured on this item (Python 3.14, one run
  in unit_sigtable.py, logged at INFO): the index of Prelude.fint (410 KB,
  1267 functions, 45 types, 49 constructors) 11 ms; the decode of one entry
  0.1 to 0.4 ms; a cold lookup after reset 0.3 ms; a warm lookup 7 us; a
  full parse of the same file 350 ms.  Tests: unit_sigtable.py (21 tests,
  both backends): the schemes of +, show, fmap, >>=, =:=, ?, unknown, Just,
  [] and _inst#Prelude.Show#[] against the printed form and against a full
  parse; Data.List, Control.SetFunctions and Data.Functor.Identity through
  curry.module; the index against a full parse on Data/Maybe.fint,
  Data/List.fint and the test module tests/data/curry/SigTable.curry (a
  class with a default, two instances, a newtype, two constraints, a
  binding without a signature, a private function); a dynamic module
  compiled with the cache off, then without its interface; the fundamental
  symbols; the instance map; the front-end check; the printer; the reset.
  Left: the start-up check runs at the first read of the Prelude interface,
  not at Interpreter.__new__, so an interpreter that never types anything
  never reads a file; the index costs 11 ms, not the 3 ms of the design's
  probe, because it indexes the types and constructors too and skips every
  quoted name; a dynamic module on a cache hit has no interface until Y3
  lands; Y4 and Y6 take the scheme objects from symbol.scheme or
  interp.sigtable.lookup, the instances from interp.sigtable.instance, and
  the declared arity of a type from interp.sigtable.type_decl.
- Y3, the interface files travel with the ICurry (issue #52).  The table of
  Y2 read the front end's copy of M.fint under .curry/pakcs-3.4.1/, which
