2026-10-05: tiered execution (B3, issue #64)
- Tiered execution on the C++ backend: the interpreter flag ``interpret``
  gets the mode 'tiered', now the default.  A module without a current
  object is interpreted at once, as under 'new' (Json2Cpp.ends_plan;
  Cpp2So.ends_plan ends the plan at a generated .cpp without an object as
  well, and loadcurry reads the JSON beside it, so a compile that was
  cancelled leaves nothing in the path), a child process compiles it in the
  background, and the runtime swaps the functions of the module to the
  compiled code when the object is ready.  The pieces: backends/cxx/
  tiered.py (the jobs, the shims, the policy, poll, wait, status, counts,
  cancel), cyrt/tiered.hpp and tiered.cpp (a detached worker thread that
  runs the commands of a job with posix_spawn, one job at a time in the
  order the modules finished loading, and the swap on the evaluating
  thread), Module::adopt, Module::keep_tables, SharedCurryModule with a
  load path, and the bindings (tiered_submit, tiered_poll, tiered_results,
  tiered_status, tiered_wait, tiered_cancel, tiered_build_shim,
  tiered_load_shim; InfoTable.address, InfoTable.has_step,
  DataType.address, Module.shlib).  The child runs sprite-make --so -z on
  the source file with the search path of the import and interpret:off;
  sprite-make sets interpret:off for the interpreter it makes, so an
  explicit compile compiles under any mode, and so does the prebuild of
  make stage.  The hooks: IBackend.module_loaded (the end of an import
  registers the shim of the module and queues the job),
  IBackend.before_evaluation and IBackend.after_evaluation
  (Interpreter.eval applies the finished jobs before an evaluation, and the
  goal applies and logs them when its values are exhausted); the scheduler
  applies them at its periodic safepoint too (check_interrupts, every 65536
  steps), so a running evaluation swaps in the middle: a step in flight
  finishes on its bytecode, the next step of the function runs compiled.
  Interpreter.stats and the --stats line gained the fields swapped (the
  functions swapped) and failed_compiles.  Policy: an expression module, an
  interactive module, and a module compiled from a string
  (str2module.is_temporary) stay interpreted; a failed compile, shim, or
  load leaves the module interpreted and is logged once per module, by the
  next poll, at the end of the evaluation, or at exit; a reset, a reload,
  and the exit of the process cancel the queue and kill the running child
  (its own process group), so no compiler outlives the interpreter and a
  short program never waits, and a program shorter than its compile ends
  interpreted with no object written; the Prelude loads compiled from the
  prebuilt object as before.
- The swap and the one-table rule.  The runtime compares info tables by
  address (graph/equality.cpp, RuntimeState::add_binding, inspect.isa), so
  the compiled object must use the tables the interpreter made.  A compiled
  object names its tables and types by ELF symbol, its own included
  (R_X86_64_GLOB_DAT in the code, R_X86_64_64 in the bill of materials and
  the constructor arrays), and the dynamic linker binds a reference to the
  first definition in the global scope.  So tiered.py registers a shim per
  module with tables made at run time when its load ends: g++ -shared
  -nostdlib over an empty file with one --defsym per table and type, the
  address of the object made at run time (21 ms), loaded RTLD_GLOBAL before
  any object (a job carries every shim not yet loaded; the loader loads
  them before an object it loads).  The dynamic initializer of a static
  table of the object constructs it at the address its symbol resolves to,
  so the load of the object writes the compiled step, the name, the format,
  and the flags into the tables of the interpreter, and the object's own
  copies stay blank.  The load is the swap; Module::adopt counts the
  functions, takes the step of a function the initializers did not reach
  from the step function's own symbol and clears its aux field, so a
  swapped table looks the same on both paths, clears the static-object flag
  the initializers wrote, and links the object.  Module keeps the tables it
  made in owned lists; a module with tables made at run time, under an
  interpreting mode, moves them to a process-wide kept registry when it is
  cleared, with its object (the kept tables hold its steps), and a module
  of the same name made later takes a kept table of the same tag and arity
  back (create_infotable, create_type), so a shim stays valid across a
  reload; a module loaded from its object has nothing to keep, and its
  object goes with it.  A module whose tables changed shape after its shim
  was made stays interpreted, and so do the modules that import it (their
  objects would bind to the old tables), with one warning.  The review of
  the first version found and this version fixed: (1) dlopen names a loaded
  object by its path, so a file written over the path of a mapped object
  (the kept object of an earlier incarnation) was never read and the swap
  installed the old code; the swap probes with RTLD_NOLOAD and loads such a
  file through a hard link of its own name beside it (a copy when the file
  system refuses the link), removed once the object is mapped;
  SharedCurryModule(sofilename, loadpath) records the canonical name and
  takes over the registry entry of the module.  (2) Once dlopen ran, the
  initializers have written the live tables, so no failure after the load
  unmaps the object: the swap opens the object itself first and never
  closes that handle on a failure path.  (3) A compiled object names the
  objects of its imports as needed libraries, which the dynamic linker
  mapped before the loader processed the imports, stale or not: an edited
  import left a stale object mapped and two tables per symbol.  Cpp2So
  .is_stale reads the imports of a .so from the generated file beside it
  and imports them first; an object is loaded only when every import was
  loaded from its object, else the module is interpreted (or compiled
  again under 'off'), and its compile waits in the queue behind the
  compile of the import.  (4) A result applied at a safepoint was never
  logged in a process that evaluates once: the end of an evaluation and
  the exit of the process now poll, and the stats line carries the counts.
  (5) The shim of a module is registered when its load ends, not computed
  for every module of the interpreter before an object loads, so a
  half-loaded module (in the registry before its symbols) no longer logs
  a spurious shape-change warning and consumes the once-key.  (6) The
  kept-table rule keeps a module only when it has tables made at run time:
  a module loaded from its object is freed with its registry entry as
  under 'off'.  (7) A kept table keeps its step on both swap paths; the
  revert to the interpreter in clear is gone.  Also found: the destructor
  of a std::condition_variable blocks until its waiters left (glibc), so a
  static one with the worker waiting on it hung the exit of every process
  that queued a job; the state of the worker is a leaked singleton.  A job
  popped before a cancel used to start anyway; a job's command starts
  under the lock after a check of the generation.  DataType.flags is a
  char in the bindings.  A re-import of a module whose C++ module is still
  held found swapped tables with a step; the materializer attaches a body
  to a table without a step only (InfoTable.has_step).
- Found on the way to the default.  The runtime headers changed, so every
  object compiled before (the test corpus included) was stale by its ABI
  stamp.  Under 'off' a test process compiled such a module again in place;
  under 'tiered' it interprets the module and the background compile dies
  with the short process, so the tests that inspect the compiled object of
  a corpus module (unit_cxx_bom, unit_cxx_literals, unit_cxx_partial,
  unit_cxx_passthrough, unit_cxx_rewrite) fail until the prepare pass of
  the runner (./run_tests --prepare) compiles the corpus; tests/README and
  AGENTS.md say so.  A test that pins 'off' by a reload must import the
  Prelude before it fakes a step of the toolchain: a new interpreter loads
  the Prelude on first use, the plan of the installed Prelude then ran
  under a mocked format stamp, took the installed .cpp for stale,
  regenerated it from the module whose C++ tables were still the static
  ones of the object (3448 extern declarations, no definition), and the
  next process compiled that stub into the installation; every file that
  loaded the Prelude failed with an undefined symbol until make stage
  rebuilt it.  Two hazards remain and are listed below.  The mixed mode
  had a gap: an interpreted module that applies readFile of the compiled
  Prelude to a nondeterministic name raised ValueError('bad Curry string')
  where compiled code and a program interpreted whole raise the
  nondeterminism error; the entry on the saturation of apply chains (O1)
  found the cause, a primitive that carried the name of its wrapper, and
  closed it.  The tests that drive the compile steps set the flag to 'off' for
  themselves: unit_cxx_toolchain.py (per test, with the Prelude imported
  first), test_values, test_private_target_across_modules,
  test_linked_against_the_target_module, and
  test_target_module_joins_the_imports of unit_optimize.py,
  test_compile_time of unit_api.py, testReusedModuleNameIsAnError of
  unit_compile.py, and test_module_in_a_package of unit_prebuild.py;
  test_split_items of unit_benchmarks.py accepts the other flags of the
  environment.
- Measured on the final build (the machine carried another stream; load
