2026-10-05: G7a, the age and phase counters of the collector (Gate 0, #81)
- Stage 0 of the generational design (the perf-program note
  gc-generational.md, section 7): counters instead of estimates.  Done:
  (W0.1) every collection records the seconds of its phases (the roots:
  the configurations of the queues and the nodes Python holds; the trace;
  the block sweep; the registries: the free-variable tables, the
  generators, the queues and sets destroyed with their configurations),
  the nodes marked, the configurations pushed, and the queues and
  configurations destroyed; gc_counters() sums them, gc_last_collection()
  holds the last record, sprite-exec --stats and curry.stats print the
  sums as fourteen gc_ keys after failed_compiles, and SPRITE_GC_REPORT=1
  prints one key=value line per collection on stderr (it replaces the
  compile-time GC_REPORT).  The timers cost a clock reading per queue
  pushed and a few per collection.  (W0.2) The sweep copies the marks of
  a collection into an ``old`` bitmap of the block header and sets a
  ``young`` flag on a block that kept no node; the mark phase counts the
  old and the young nodes it marks; the bitmap is an input of the
  counters only, the trace is unchanged, and the verifier checks that the
  old bits lie within the allocated slots, on an explicit request and in
  the stress mode at every collection (a pass over the blocks, as the
  sweep makes).  gc_old_node_count() reads the bitmap.  (W0.3) The
  old-write counters: a test at every return of a step in procS (the
  redex; inline, the young flag of the block first, the bit of the slot
  second: src/cyrt/graph/gc/block.hpp, the layout of a block shared by
  blockheap.cpp and the scheduler) and at the slot sites of the design
  (the compressions of forward chains in Variable::skip,
  Node::successor_node and compress_fwd_chain, the generator slot and the
  bindings of a free variable, the string advance of writeFile,
  Variable::set_successor, Cursor::skipfwd, and the forward_to,
  set_successor and step entries of the bindings), counting the writes
  into old nodes, the distinct old nodes written (a ``dirty`` bit per
  slot, counted and cleared by the sweep: the unit a write barrier
  records) and the blocks touched per interval; a slot whose node is not
  at hand is looked up in a two-level heap map over the 47-bit address
  space (in_heap_map, a bit per block; a block above that space stays
  unmapped and uncounted), because the address may be a Configuration
  field, a local, or an interpreter cell.  Compiled in under
  SPRITE_GC_WRITE_COUNTERS (make GC_WRITE_COUNTERS=1); without the macro
  the three hooks are empty inline functions, so the default build has
  no call at the sites and the indexer stays inline in generated code.
  Some sites are in the installed headers, so the setting reaches the
  installation as sysconfig/cxx_gc_write_counters
  (config.cxx_gc_write_counters) and the generated modules through the
  compile flags and the ABI stamp (toolchain.gc_flags, object_digest):
  a switch compiles the modules again.  The cost on Tak1 (perf stat
  -e instructions:u, three runs per build within 0.01 percent, the
  module compiled): the base install 16,271 M, the default build 16,259
  M (-0.07 percent), the instrumented build 17,483 M, 7.45 percent over
  the base, 36 instructions per step, over the one-percent budget of the
  design: beside the redex test, every Bool result is a forward node to a
  pinned constructor, and the case that consumes it compresses the
  chain, so the slot sites run about once per tak call.  The first
  version declared the hooks out of line in every build; the review
  measured its default build at 17,097 M, 5.09 percent over the base,
  because the empty calls made g++ outline Variable::skip in the module.
  Found on the way: g++ takes the first precompiled-header member of
  cyrt.hpp.gch/ whose options agree with the compilation and never
  compares a member with the headers, so a compilation that defines a
  macro the current member was built without (this one, or
  SPRITE_GC_MPS) took a stale member of an earlier runtime, and the
  module was compiled against old headers; PrecompiledHeader.prepare now
  removes the stale members of the other flavors (remove_stale_members).
  (W0.4) The runs, through the harness with the modules compiled, two
  measured repetitions and one under perf, on a machine shared with four
