2026-10-05: O1, saturation of apply chains (#70)
- The pass saturate_applies (interpreter/optimize.py; the analysis in
  icurry/analysis/partials.py) runs before inline_aliases under the key
  <backend>.opt.saturate_applies.  An apply whose head is known becomes
  the call it builds.  A head is known when it is a partial application
  (IFPCall, or ICPCall of a constructor), a call without arguments of a
  nullary function whose body is one, or an apply whose head is known and
  whose call is a partial application, followed through at most eight
  nullary functions and applies (analysis.partial_head).  The last form is
  the body of a nullary function the pass has not reached yet (g = k 1
  with k = f defined after the use), so the result does not depend on the
  order of the functions in the module.  A call of Prelude.$, the
  Prelude's alias of apply (analysis.is_apply), is an apply to the pass,
  which runs before inline_aliases.  A user alias of apply (app f x = f x)
  is not: the source wrote the indirection, and a program may need it to
  hand a known function value to the apply of the runtime, which the pass
  would fold away (CxxPartial.curry does, for the shared function-value
  node and the partials of 32 to 35 arguments; a let of a bare function
  value does not survive the front end, so no variable can hold one).
  inline_aliases makes an apply of such a call afterwards, on a head the
  pass would have known.  The applies are rewritten
  from the inside out, one at a time (analysis.saturate): an apply of a
  partial that misses one argument is the saturated call (IFCall, or
  ICCall), one of a partial that misses more is the longer partial, and a
  saturated call is not a known head, so the applies after it stay; a
  saturated call that is itself an apply (ap = apply, then ap f x) is
  saturated again.  So one rule covers the exact, the under-saturated, and
  the over-saturated chain.  The arguments a saturation copies from the
  body of a nullary function are rewritten too.  The arity comes from
  the symbol table of the module of the head and must agree with the
  missing count of the partial: the runtime derives the missing count of a
  partial with arguments from the arity (Node::create_partial), and a call
  built from a partial that disagreed would have the wrong size.  A
  variable head, a call with arguments (the method selectors applied to a
  dictionary, 343 sites of the corpus), and a head held in a variable stay.
  The body of a nullary function is copied into the use site, as its step
  would build it there, once per use, so a choice in it stays one choice
  per use.  Each nullary function records the partial it unfolds to under
  all.unfolding as ICurry-JSON (analysis.UNFOLDING_KEY), so a module loaded
  from its compiled form tells it to its importers.  When the copied body
  names a symbol of a module the caller does not import, the module joins
  the imports.  Prelude: 942 apply calls, 263 with a known head (134
  whose head is a partial application or a nullary function, and the
  applies above them in a chain), and 3 calls of $ with a known head (chr $
  and lex $), become 679 apply calls, none with a known head, and no call
  of $; 120 nullary functions record an unfolding (115 of functions, 5 of
  constructors: userError = UserError, showChar, the pure and return of
  Maybe and Either), 117 distinct texts.  The heads are the _impl#
  methods that fall back to a class default (_impl#/=#Prelude.Eq#
  Prelude.Int = _def#/=#Prelude.Eq _inst#Prelude.Eq#Prelude.Int).  In
  Queens10, x /= q in safe was apply (apply (_impl#/= []) x) q, three
  times, and is _def#/=#Prelude.Eq _inst#Eq#Int x q; isdivs of Primes
  likewise, with _def#mod#Prelude.Integral inside.
- Staleness: the format stamps went to 11 (C++) and 5 (Python), so make
  stage and the first import write every cached module again, once (9 and
  3, then 10 and 4, were earlier forms of the pass in the working tree,
  never committed); a file of the old format is correct but slower, and
  its record tells the modules that import it no unfoldings.  The key of a
  pass changes no stamp by itself: a module compiled before the pass is
  current by its ABI stamp and by its format, and would run as it was
  compiled.
- Found on the way, one: the C++ loader built the IModule of a shared
  object with the metadata {cxx.shlib} alone, so the keys of the passes,
  which the record carries, never reached the module, and every pass ran
  again over the bodiless functions of every compiled module at every
  import (five passes over the 1670 functions of the Prelude).  The loader
  hands the keys of the passes on now (backends/cxx/loader.py), as the
  Python backend does: the Prelude import takes 0.027 s in place of
  0.055 s, and every program retires fewer instructions at start (Fib
  0.92x).  Before the fix the new pass had added about 80 million
  instructions to every process (Fib 1.03x), which the first measurement
  showed.  The merge key cxx.is_merged stays behind: the merge adds the
  exported primitives, which the record does not list (unit_prelude.py,
  unit_inspect.py and unit_io_bind.py failed with it restored).
- Found on the way, two: the record of a shared object names a function by
  the name field of its table, and fourteen primitives of the runtime
  carried the name of the Curry function that wraps them (showIntLiteral
  for prim_showIntLiteral; the show and read literals, error, putChar,
  readFile, writeFile, appendFile, ioError).  The two rows collided, in
  Module::link and in IModule.fromBOM, and the primitive took the
  wrapper's place in the symbol table of the loaded Prelude.  Compiled code
  never noticed: it binds the wrapper by its C++ symbol.  The pass copies
  _impl#showsPrec#Prelude.Show#Prelude.Int = showSigned _inst#Real#Int
  (showString . showIntLiteral) into the modules that call showsPrec at
  Int, and an interpreted module then named Prelude.showIntLiteral through
  the symbol table and got the primitive, which shows its argument without
  normalizing it: showsPrec 11 (-2.5) "" gave "(-apply (negate ...) (-2.5))"
  (unit_show_float.py).  The primitives carry their Curry names now
  (src/cyrt/currylib/prelude/{show,read,basic,io}.cpp); an unevaluated
  application of one prints as prim_showIntLiteral x.  The same collision
  was the "gap of the interpreter in the mixed mode" of the tiered entry
  above: an interpreted module that applied readFile of the compiled
  Prelude to a nondeterministic name reached prim_readFile, which raised
  ValueError on the unevaluated name; it reaches the wrapper now, whose $##
  raises the nondeterminism error, and test_nondet_io_argument_error in
  unit_cxx_tiered.py is no longer an expected failure.  The loader asserts
  for every module, the Prelude included, that the table, the record, and
  the ICurry agree on the name of a function (interpreter/import_/load.py;
  the Prelude was exempt, which let the collision through), so a new
  collision fails at load.  The import of the Prelude from its ICurry
  (interpret 'all', and the compile of the Prelude itself) then found one
  more table with a name of its own: the table of nonstrictEq said
  nonStrictEq (prelude/constr.cpp); it carries the Curry name now.  Tests:
  TestFunctionNames in unit_cxx_bom.py (the fourteen pairs, no two rows of
