2026-10-05: example 24, a build system (the decisions of make as a relation)
- examples/24-build-system, the opener of the talk narrative (the notes
  of 2026-10-05) and the example of section 12.2 of the phase 3 plan.
  Curry owns the decisions, Build.curry (about 150 lines): rulesFor is
  one equation per rule, the pattern rule %.o: %.c as the functional
  pattern stem ++ ".o", the library and the program by name, and the
  explicit rules of the project through free variables and =:= over
  anyOf; rulesOf collects the rules of a target with set2 and sortValues,
  and the rest of the module reads the rules as plain data; inputs
  follows the include table Python scanned through the headers with a
  seen list; stale is a relation over a table of (file, stamp) pairs;
  waves and layers are the topological order, a cycle fails the guard
  and the plan has no value; ready is the one-line scheduler; affected
  is the relation read backwards (t = anyOf (targets p) under the guard
  reads p t f); ambiguous is the lint, a target with two rules.  An
  explicit rule for a new name adds a target.  Python owns the world,
  go.py: a four-source C project in a temporary directory, include
  scanning by a regular expression, stamps as st_mtime_ns, a thread pool
  that submits the ready set and asks Curry again after each completion
  (Python keeps the running set and the outcome of each recipe), the
  recipes through CC or cc and ar with a stand-in that writes placeholder
  files when no compiler is on the PATH (the same stdout, a note on
  stderr), --trace, --jobs, and --serve PORT with http.server and a JSON
  endpoint.  The tested run: the full build, a build with nothing to do,
  a touch of shape.h with the prediction of affected (the build rebuilds
  exactly that set), a second rule for main.o (the lint stops the
  build), and a rule that closes a cycle (the plan has no value).  The
  docs page of the examples has its section; unit_examples.py runs it on
  both backends.
- Measured on this machine, under the 2 GB cap: warm ./run 0.42 to 0.46 s
  on the C++ backend (three runs) and 8.3 s on the Python backend (two
  runs); a first run from a copy without products, with the cache off,
  1.2 s and 8.7 s; without a compiler on the PATH 0.35 s and 8.2 s.  The
  Python backend answers a question in 0.1 to 0.7 s (the ready set on a
  built tree 0.37 s, the plan 0.71 s, the stale check of one target
  0.17 s; the C++ backend at most 0.03 s), and the run asks dozens of
  them (the build loop asks for the ready set once per completion), which
  is nearly all of its time.
