2026-10-05: set functions, the repair at the escape (#61, the general case)
- The defect left by the bug batch: a set function that starts while its
  free variable is unbound, is consumed in part, and resumes under
  alternatives that narrowed the variable differently shared the SetEval
  node and its queue; the nested queue read the fingerprint of the
  alternative that resumed it first (choice_escapes refused the escape of a
  choice an enclosing configuration had made, and the nested fork pruned it
  by that fingerprint), and the shared allValues node kept that value for
  the other alternative: narrowedAfterStart gave [A, A] twice.
- The repair, on both backends, is rule SF.1 of the dissertation (chapter
  4, Figure 4.2) at the escape.  A choice of an escape set always leaves
  the capsule: choice_escapes (rts_setfunctions.cpp, rts_setfunctions.py)
  reads no enclosing fingerprint any more, and no fingerprint at all; it
  escapes a choice that is in the escape set of the current set or of an
  enclosing set, unless the queue has escaped it before.  The split
  (Queue::split, split_queue) keeps a configuration that made the choice
  LEFT, moves one that made it RIGHT, keeps one that has not made it in
  both queues, as before, and records the choice in both queues
  (Queue::decisions, Queue.decisions), so a configuration meets it a second
  time without an escape: it forks, and the fork reads the side from its
  own fingerprint or, through the walk of the queue stack, from the
  enclosing configuration, which runs this queue because it decided the
  choice that way (the queue of a side is reachable through that side of
  the escaped choice alone).  The escaped choice is a node of the shared
  graph like any pull-tabbed choice: an enclosing configuration that has
  not decided it forks on it, and one that has prunes it.  A configuration
  that has decided it (itself, or an enclosing one did: read_fp) takes its
  side at once through a private copy of its spine and E_RESTART
  (allValues_step, allValues), as a binding goes into an expression; the
  fork would prune the choice node to the same side, but it sends the
  configuration to the back of its queue, and a search with many
  alternatives then advances them in lockstep with every capsule alive.
  The review of this entry (below) bounded that fast path: a configuration
  takes the side at once only when it owns the decision.  The capsule
  writes nothing into the shared graph that depends on the configuration
  that runs it; the walk of fork and read_fp through the queue stack stays,
  and it prunes by an enclosing decision only in a queue that an escape of
  the choice has bound to that side.
- Decided first, the captured choice (test_captured_argument of
  unit_cxx_scheduler.py, choiceCaptured of unit_setfunctions_bugs.py): the
  call-time choice reading stays.  A choice captured through the function
  position (set1 (constT x) 0 with x = A ? B) is in no escape set; the
  dissertation's SF.1 reads the escape set of the capsule alone, and a
  captured occurrence is unboxed, so the rule encapsulates it; Sprite's
  nested fork prunes it by the fingerprint of the enclosing configuration
  when that configuration decided it before the capsule reached it, which
  gives (A, [A]) and (B, [B]), and encapsulates it otherwise.  The goldens
  of applyS.curry (from KiCS2) pin the same reading: an argument applied
  with $< is encapsulated (goal1, goal4, goal30), and a captured occurrence
  follows the guarded one of the same choice (goal10, goal11).  PAKCS
  cannot check these goals: the pinned installation has no setfunctions
  package.  nestedCapture is the other half: the inner function captures
  the argument of the outer set function, so the choice is in the escape
  set of the outer set alone; SF.1 read literally keeps it in the inner
  capsule ([[1, 2]]), which puts the non-determinism of the outer argument
  inside a value of the outer set function.  A choice of the escape set of
  an enclosing set came through the guard of an argument of that set
  function, so it is from outside that capsule and from outside every
  capsule nested in it: choice_escapes reads the escape sets of the
  enclosing sets as well, and nestedCapture gives [[1]] and [[2]]; the
  expected-failure mark of test_captured_argument is gone.  goal10 of
  applyS.curry decided the last detail: a configuration that decided an
  escape-set choice itself (through the captured occurrence, before the
  guarded one put the choice into the escape set) still escapes it, and the
  split sorts it to its side; a refusal kept the two sides of the argument
  in one set.
- The creation-time walk (holds_private_state, _holds_private_state) stays
  unchanged.  The captured shapes need it: without the private copy the
  shared capsule of choiceCaptured or viaPartial keeps the values of the
  configuration that created it.  The guarded shapes (boundVar, choiceArg)
  no longer need it: their choice escapes, and a variable the capsule
  returns is resolved by each reader of the value; there the copy only
  loses the sharing of the capsule.  The walk could skip the guarded
  subtrees; not done, because the shapes that stay shared are exactly the
  ones the escape then has to handle, and the gain is one capsule per
  alternative in programs that narrow an argument before the set function
  sees it.
- Two forms were measured and rejected on the way (QueensSet9, C++
  backend, one repetition, the HEAD build 8be3209c of the same machine
  as the reference: 12146105 steps, 2979683 forks, 11 collections,
  766038 configurations destroyed, 58100 queues destroyed, 18.17 G
  instructions, 338 MB peak).  A split that clones every undecided
  configuration at once, LEFT in the kept queue and RIGHT in the new one:
  923263 configurations destroyed, 461 MB, instructions +6.2 percent; the
  clones of the side nobody runs are the cost, where the lazy clone at the
  step (unshare_front) never paid for them.  A lazy split that writes the
  side into the fingerprint of the clone: steps 12146105 to 12328580 in
  its first form (a configuration cloned by one queue stayed undecided in
  the other, which held it alone and escaped the choice again), and with
  that fixed through a count of the decisions applied, 19.67 G
  instructions (+8.3 percent) and 413 MB: the writes of a sparse id into
  every nested fingerprint, which the walk made unnecessary in the first
  place.  The final form writes nothing: the queue records the choice,
  choice_escapes looks it up (a vector of the escaped choices of the
  lineage, 36 entries at most here).
- Measured with the harness (C++ backend, one repetition, other work on
  the machine, after against the HEAD build): QueensSet 2152241 steps,
  511428 forks, 3 collections; QueensSet9 12146105, 2979683, 11; PermSort
  12531076, 1588167, 9: all three equal before and after, the
  configurations and queues destroyed equal (QueensSet 95535 and 8911,
