- Review of the entry, applied.  (1) The repair above left two leaks of
  the same class, both found with probes on both backends.  A captured
  choice decided after the start (`let x = A ? B in let vs = valuesOf
  (set2 pickOrCall A (constT x)) in (head vs =:= A) &> (x, vs)` with
  `pickOrCall z f = z ? f 0`) gave (A, [A, A]) and (B, [A, A]): the choice
  is in no escape set, so the nested fork pruned it by the fingerprint of
  the alternative that ran the capsule first, inside the capsule both
  alternatives share, and update_fp writes nothing into the nested
  fingerprint when the walk decided.  The same with a captured variable
  ([A, A] twice) and with the captured occurrence before the guarded one
  (`pickOrBoth z f y = [z] ? [f 0, y]`: (B, [[A], [A, B]]), the captured
  element from the A world).  And a guarded shape two levels deep: an
  outer capsule alive and shared whose configuration consumes the second
  value of an inner capsule over the variable after the outside narrowed
  it (`inner3 y = let ws = valuesOf (set2 pickOrCase A y) in head ws ?
  (ws !! 1)`, `set1 inner3 u`) gave [A, A] twice: the escape from the
  inner capsule reached the configuration of the outer one, and the fast
  path took the side that read_fp found in the outside through the walk,
  into the private spine of a configuration that both alternatives of the
  outside share.  Two rules close them, on both backends.  choice_escapes
  escapes a choice in no escape set as well when an enclosing
  configuration has decided it (the walk over the enclosing fronts, the
  fingerprint of the nested configuration itself unread); a choice no
  enclosing configuration decided is encapsulated as before.  And the fast
  path of allValues_step and allValues takes the side at once only when
  the configuration owns the decision (RuntimeState::owns_decision,
  owns_decision): it made the choice itself, or its queue was split on the
  choice (Queue::decided).  A side that an enclosing configuration alone
  decided is not taken: the choice node stays, reaches the root of the
  configuration, and escapes in turn, up to the configuration that decided
  it, so every capsule between them is split and each world runs its own
  side.  The invariant that results: the walk of fork and read_fp prunes
  by an enclosing decision only in a queue that an escape of the choice
  has bound to that side.  The call-time reading of the captured shapes
  holds (choiceCaptured, goal10 and goal11 of applyS.curry), and the
  creation-time copy of holds_private_state stays: for a captured choice
  decided before the start it is the cheaper path, one capsule per
  alternative instead of a split and a restart per choice.  Tests in
  TestResumedCapsule: the captured choice, the captured variable, the
  captured occurrence before the guarded one, and the outer capsule alive
  over the variable and over a choice.  (2) The enclosing-set escape rule
  had its only test in a class the Python backend skips; nestedCaptureCase
  of CxxScheduler.curry (the capture under a case, so the guard of the
  outer set is below the root) is the shape both backends run, in
  TestSetGuardRoot.  (3) The comment of fork said the walk reads nothing
  for an escape-set choice; it reads it after the split, which the split
  relies on: reworded.  (4) The for header of choice_escapes was over 79
  columns: wrapped.  (5) The factor in Overloads.curry (275) did not match
  the measured steps (117757458 against 425202, 277): the comment says
  about 280 and quotes the counts.  (6) rts.hpp declared filter_queue
  without a definition since the Python twin became split_queue: removed.
- Measured after the review (C++ backend, one repetition, the same
