2026-10-06: the binding optimization rewrites the FlatCurry file (#85)
- Cause.  Since 1b554fd1 the build route applied the pass in memory
  (translate_file(..., bindingopt=True)) while the icurry route and the
  oracle translated the file as the front end wrote it: the two routes
  wrote different .icy files for a module with a required Boolean
  equality, and the on-disk oracle check accepted a product of the build
  route only through a second translation (accept_build_route, 4e21df99).
  PAKCS applies the optimization to the FlatCurry file itself:
  preprocessFcyFile in compiler.pl runs BindingOpt, which writes M.fcy_OPT
  and renames it over M.fcy when an equality was replaced.
- Fix.  A FlatCurry writer in the format of the front end, the show of
  Haskell (terms.showhaskell; flatcurry.show and flatcurry.write).  The
  PAKCS writer showterm does not serve: the front end writes Prog "M" ...
  without the outer parentheses, "" for the empty string, the ASCII names
  \NUL to \DEL and \SO\&H in a string, and 1.0e7 for a float.  The writer
  round-trips every .fcy of the corpus byte for byte, the 1260 files
  under tests/data and the 1210 files of the archive, the library
  included, on its first run; func_flat2icurry.py pins it
  (test_writer_round_trip), and unit_flat2icurry.py tests the escapes,
  the numbers and the parentheses (TestHaskellWriter) and the sample, the
  probes and the library modules of the archive (TestFlatCurryWriter).
  The rewrite step, bindingopt.optimize_file: both routes run it after
  the front end and before the translation (_frontend.curry2icurry; the
  icurry branch of Curry2ICurryConverter.convert runs the front end and
  the rewrite before the icurry program, whose own front-end run finds
  its files current and leaves the rewritten file alone, checked by the
  time of the file).  The file is written again when the pass replaced
  an equality, as transbooleq writes it; a rule whose equalities all stay
  is rebuilt (its $ reduced) only then, so optimize_bindings returns the
  program itself when the count is zero (the Prelude is such a program:
  no replacement, a rebuilt rule).  The translation runs with
  bindingopt=False on both routes, with icurry_compat as before.  The
  pass is idempotent over the archive: a second run replaces nothing and
  gives the same program, and the optimized program survives the writer
  and the reader (test_rewrite_is_idempotent).  The cache digest of the
  icurry route covers the sources of the pass (cache.REWRITE_SOURCES).
- Decisions.  --bindingopt of python -m curry.toolchain.flat2icurry stays
  the in-memory pass: it translates a file the routes have not rewritten
  as the routes would, and leaves the file alone.  Two hand runs:
  python -m curry.toolchain.flat2icurry.rewrite M.fcy... rewrites
  FlatCurry files in place (a module of its own, so python -m gives no
  warning about a module the package imported), and sprite-make
  --rewrite-flat M runs the step from Curry to ICurry again, current or
  not, without the ICurry cache (a hit writes no FlatCurry), and makes
  the ICurry and the later products from the rewritten file; it runs in
  this process (--jobs 1) and needs the source.
- The oracle.  test_products_on_disk runs without the fallback;
  accept_build_route is gone.  Before the change, with the fallback off,
  two of the 1258 pairs on disk in this worktree differed, CxxInterp and
  CxxScheduler: an .icy of the in-memory pass beside the front end's
  .fcy.  No product differed through icurry_compat alone, so no fallback
  for that setting is kept.  The hand run rewrote the two files (one
  equality each), and the check passes.  The pass changes 7 of the 1260
  files under tests/data (CxxInterp, CxxScheduler, atableNoflex,
  sendMoreMoney with 15 equalities, BFSvsPar, RegExp,
  poker_four_of_a_kind) and some files of the archive.
- What stays as it is.  The committed ICurry of the Curry library
  predates the pass and is not regenerated: make stage derives the JSON
  from the committed .icy and never translates a library module unless
  SPRITE_REBUILD_ICY=1; that run would now rewrite the library's .fcy
  under curry/lib/.curry and translate the optimized program.  The
  archive's .fcy files are the front end's and pair with the .icy files
  icurry wrote from them; a rebuilt archive packs rewritten .fcy files
  with icurry's translation of them.  A product written before this
  change keeps its .fcy as the front end wrote it until the source is
  newer (the front end then writes the file again and the route rewrites
  it) or a hand run rewrites it; an .icy of the pre-pass era is remade
  only by sprite-make --rewrite-flat or a newer source.  In this worktree
  the .fcy and the .icy of sendMoreMoney under tests/data are both
  unoptimized (made before the pass) and stay so; the test compiles the
  program from text.  A .fcy that PAKCS rewrote in place (the oracle
  runs PAKCS on the test modules, and its BindingOpt writes the showTerm
  format) is not the front end's text: the reader reads it, the pass
  finds nothing to replace, and the round-trip test leaves such a file
  out; the corpus holds none today.
- Verification, one file per process under prlimit --as 6 GB, both
  backends: unit_flat2icurry.py 77 OK (2 skipped),
  unit_flat2icurry_bindingopt.py 31 OK, unit_curry2icurry.py 15 OK (the
  icurry route is configured in this install, so its test ran: both
  routes write the same bytes for sendMoreMoney, 15 constrEq),
  func_flat2icurry.py 6 OK, unit_py_residuation.py 3 OK.  The probes:
  the writer over the 2470 files of the corpus and the archive, none
  differ; the icurry route on a fresh copy of sendMoreMoney gives the
  bytes of the port and leaves the rewritten .fcy with its time;
  sprite-make --rewrite-flat --json over a module whose .fcy was put
  back to the front end's text rewrites the .fcy and the .icy and writes
  the JSON, and refuses --jobs 2 and a module without a source.
  tests/manifest.json has no entry for unit_flat2icurry_bindingopt.py;
  unit_curry2icurry.py has 15 tests now (11 recorded) and takes about
  25 s more for the test of the icurry route.
- Open.  Pairs of other worktrees and machines made in the in-memory era
  differ in test_products_on_disk until the hand run; the docstring of
  the test names the two commands.  The archive is not rebuilt here, and
  the library is not regenerated here.
- Review follow-ups.  The icurry route needs the front end as well now (it
  runs it first and rewrites its FlatCurry file), so a missing front end
  is reported by Curry2ICurryConverter.command before any program runs,
  with the real requirement, and _frontend.command no longer recommends
  the icurry route in its message.  configure requires the front end
  whatever --with-icurry says (set_frontend; set_curry2icurry_tool aborts
  with the reason when it is left out), and the configure page says so.
  The digest of the icurry route covers the front-end flags too
  (cache.frontend_digest): the file icurry translates is the one Sprite's
  run of the front end wrote with them (icurry finds it current), so a
  change of CURRY_FRONTEND_FLAGS changed the output of the route under an
  unchanged key.  Tests: test_tool_selection of unit_curry2icurry.py (both
  messages, the front end mocked away), test_front_end_required_with_icurry
  of unit_configure.py, and test_route_is_part_of_the_key of unit_cache.py
  (the icurry branch when icurry is configured).

2026-10-06: potholes (#63, #86, example 21 on the Python backend, #62)
- #63, a point-free definition applied to arguments from Python.  Cause:
  the untyped builder (ExpressionBuilder in expressions.py, behind raw_expr
  and expr with typed_expr off) counted the arguments against the arity
  alone: fewer gave a partial application, more went to make_node as they
  were, where the Python backend raised TypeError and the C++ binding
  dropped the surplus in silence (Node_create handed the vector to
  Node::create, which packs by the format string and never counts).  The
  typed builder of Y7 already folds apply over the surplus, so
  curry.eval(M.norm, 3) and curry.expr gave 7 on both backends before the
  change; raw_expr gave TypeError on the Python backend and the partial
  application norm on the C++ backend.  Fix: the untyped builder applies
  the head to its arity and folds Prelude.apply over the rest, one node
  per argument, the target on the last, as the typed builder does; a
  symbol that is not a function applied to a surplus raises
  CurryTypeError with the sentence of the typed builder, without the
  scheme ("Just takes 1 argument, 2 given", where the typed builder says
  "Just :: a -> Maybe a takes 1 argument, 2 given"; the review found the
  two builders with two sentences for one mistake); Node_create checks the
  count against the arity, equal or
  below it for a partial application, and raises TypeError with the
  message of the Python backend; create_partial_applic of the Python
  backend runs the count check before its assertion, so a partial
  application with too many arguments raises TypeError on both backends.
  Tests: TestSurplusArguments of unit_expr.py on both backends (norm =
  (+ 1) . (* 2) through raw_expr, expr and eval; twice applied once and
  twice; a constructor with a surplus through both builders; make_node
  with two, zero and one too many arguments).  The docstring of raw_expr
  names the rule.
- #86, a name produced by a functional pattern stays narrowed.  Measured
  with sprite-exec --stats on the C++ backend, step mode, a module with
  source (stem ++ ".o") = stem ++ ".c", the same read through a set
  function (sourceS t = case sortValues (set1 source t) of [s] -> s), and
  the same with the result evaluated to normal form (sourceN t = id $##
  source t).  One comparison: 151, 148 and 168 steps, 8 forks each.  One
  name compared 200 times (the name shared, the comparisons not): 222141
  steps and 1202 forks through source, 14723 steps and 8 forks through
  sourceS, 180362 steps and 1202 forks through sourceN.  nub over 49
  names: 944283 steps and 9800 forks, 28308 and 392, 1048653 and 14210.
  The list of distinct names read twice: 1889471 and 19796, 29094 and
  392, 1705647 and 28616.  The Python backend forks the same (1202
  against 8) and takes 2.36 s against 0.27 s for the 200 comparisons; it
  counts a rewrite alone (15484 against 15503 steps).  Cause: a binding
  and a narrowing are private to the configuration that made them, so a
  read of a bound variable, or of a narrowed one, copies the spine of the
  configuration from its root to the variable with the binding or the
  decided generator in the copy and restarts the evaluation from the root
  (replace_freevar and Scan::copy_spine of the C++ runtime; N and hnf of
  fairscheme.py).  The restart repeats the steps of every function on the
  spine, and procS counts each (the inflated steps); a decided generator
  choice met again is pull-tabbed and forked to its one side (the
  forks).  Nothing of the copy goes back into the shared graph: the next
  reader of the shared name starts over.  The pattern variables of a
  functional pattern are exactly such variables: the front end writes
  source x = cond ((stem ++ ".o") =:<= x) (stem ++ ".c") with stem free,
  =:<= narrows stem by its generator and binds each character, and the
  result holds them.  $## does not help, because its normal form lives in
  the private spine as well.  A set function helps, because the capsule
  has its own queue and root: the spine copies stay inside it, its
  configuration reaches a value in which every variable is resolved, and
  allValues_step writes that value in place as plain data.  Options and
  cost.  (1) Write the binding into the shared graph when the
  configuration owns the node: unsound without an ownership test, and an
  epoch stamp per node does not give one, because an in-place rewrite of a
  shared redex publishes a young node to every configuration.  (2) A
  per-configuration map from a copied spine node to its private copy,
  consulted at every descent, so a second reference to a copied node
  within one configuration finds the copy: restores sharing and removes
  the repeated normalization, but not the copy per read; a flag bit per
  node to make the test cheap, copy-on-write of the map at a fork, and
  the collector's view of the map: several days.  (3) A ground copy of a
  rule's result once a functional pattern matched: a change of
  strictness (the result may hold an infinite or undefined part) and the
  copy would still be private to the configuration.  (4) The idiom: read
  the result through a set function.  Taken: (4), with the measured
  reason in the primer (CurryPrimer/Syntax.rst, the note under functional
  patterns), in section 5 of the README of example 24, and in
  tests/unit_funcpat_narrowed.py, both backends: the same values by every
  route, the forks of the direct route and of the $## route growing with
  the comparisons, those of the set-function route staying at the match,
  and on the C++ backend the steps of the direct route above five times
  the steps of the set-function route.  A runtime change that removes the
  cost flips the assertions on the direct route, which is the point of
  the test.  Open: option (2) is the runtime work if the cost matters
  beyond the idiom.  (5) The review proposed the shortcut of the #61 fix
  (holds_private_state): when the outermost queue holds one configuration
  and no queue encloses it, write the binding, the generator and the
  chosen side of a decided choice in place instead of copy_spine and a
  restart.  Measured on the Python backend with the evaluator patched in
  a probe (N and hnf of fairscheme.py: the write goes to the slot of the
  parent at the cursor, under the condition and with no set guard on the
  path; the tree is unchanged), module of unit_funcpat_narrowed.py: many
  through source 15483 steps, 1202 forks, 2.22 s before; 15483 steps, 7
  forks, 0.52 s with the shortcut; 15502 steps, 8 forks, 0.13 s through
  sourceS.  distinct: 324 forks, 0.09 s before; 62 forks, 0.05 s with
  the shortcut; 72 forks, 0.02 s through the set function.  The values
  are unchanged.  The forks go, but the binding is still resolved at
  every read: 1201 writes for the 200 comparisons, because hnf meets the
  bound character at the slot of the redex (eqChar x 'a'), which is
  private to the comparison, while the holder of x, the cons cell of the
  name, keeps the variable; the configuration owns the binding, not the
  node.  Forwarding the variable node and the decided choice node
  themselves (a second variant of the probe) brings the writes down to
  12 and leaves the time at 0.54 s: the rest is the non-strict
  constraints that =:<= left in the data, lifted to the root (a spine
  copy) at every read; the set function leaves plain data.  Besides, a
  forwarded variable breaks the invariant that the variable table maps a
  vid to a free variable node (get_freevar, is_void, constrain_equal),
  and every in-place publication of configuration state is visible to a
  later evaluation of a shared subexpression (the #61 shortcut shares
  that property).  So (5) removes the forks and four fifths of the time
  on the Python backend and stops short of the set-function route; the
  C++ runtime (replace_freevar and the choice path of hnf) was not
  changed, and the idiom stays.
- Example 21, scenario (d) on the Python backend.  Cause: apply_gnf, the
  step of $## (backends/py/currylib/prelude/apply.py), checked for
  residuals before the normalization ran: _applyspecial is a generator,
  and its body, the normalization, ran only when Node consumed it after
  the check; the check walked the argument as it stood, through the
  logical successors of a Variable, which include the generator of a free
  variable, and counted every void variable it met.  In scenario (d) the
  argument was the narrowed type variable t, and the walk went through
  its generator into the Class branch this alternative had not taken,
  down to the generator of the name string and the tail variable of its
  cons branch, which no alternative ever bound (the path, printed from the
  suspended capsule: COMPLEXCASE0[0] / _Free[1] / _Choice[2] x3 /
  Class[1] / _Free[1] / _Choice[1] / :[1]); every configuration of the
  capsule suspended on that variable, and ready() raised
  EvaluationSuspended out of the nested queue.  Fix: the step runs the
  normalization first (the list of the generator), then walks the normal
  form without entering a generator and suspends on the free variables
  without a generator, the rule of applygnf_step in the C++ runtime.
  Result: depth 1 gives "60 types; after the lvalue transformations, 12:
  ..." on the Python backend; ./run with backend:py takes 24.7 s and
  matches expected.out; the backend set of the example in
  unit_examples.py is {'py', 'cxx'}, and sections 3 and 5 of its README
  say so.  Test: TestNormalFormOfNarrowedVariable of
  unit_py_residuation.py on both backends ($## over a variable narrowed
  to a value with a String field, alone and inside a set function, and
  the free-name shape that gives its value and then suspends on both
  backends, as the C++ backend did before).  The review doubted that the
  reduced shapes pin the defect, after an earlier claim that they pass
  with the old step too.  Measured again with the committed apply.py
  loaded beside the tree and its apply_gnf installed as the step of $##:
  pick 1, picks and the new pickFirst 1 did not end within 20 s on the
  Python backend, before and after the ready() fix below; with the new
  step each ends at once.  So the shapes do pin the defect.  pickFirst,
  added to NARROWED, reaches the step with the bare narrowed variable, the
  condition of the entry: ($##) comes first, suspends on the free
  variable, and the narrowing in the other conjunct of (&) wakes it.
- ready() of the Python backend read the head of the queue.  _make_ready
  filtered the residuals of a configuration with rts.is_void and no
  configuration, so has_binding and is_narrowed read rts.C, the blocked
  head, for every configuration behind it.  A configuration behind a head
  that held a binding of its residual was released, its step suspended
  again at once, and the two took turns without end:
  ((v =:= 1) &> (u + 1)) ? (v + 1) with u, v free spun at full load
  (timeout at 40 s) where the C++ runtime, whose _make_ready passes the
