- #62, list functions over about a thousand elements on the Python
  backend.  The recursion is not in hnf alone: a step function, generated
  or built in, calls hnf on its inductive argument from the middle of its
  body, and hnf calls the step of that redex: length [1..2000] ends in
  RecursionError with 16386 frames, eight per element (step_tracer, S,
  the step of plusInt, apply_unboxed, _unbox, hnf_or_free twice, hnf).
  An iterative evaluator would have every step hand its demand back to a
  driver and resume after it, a rewrite of the generated code and of the
  built-in steps: not under a day.  The first decision of this entry
  left the limit of 16384 frames and documented it, with the reason that
  the chain re-enters C at every step and the C stack bounds it.  The
  review measured otherwise, and the measurement was repeated: with
  generate_values under a limit of ten million frames, on the main thread
  with its 8 MB stack and under prlimit --as 2 GB, length over 10000
  elements passes in 0.6 s (76 MB peak), 40000 in 2.8 s (175 MB), 160000
  in 10.8 s (575 MB, 1.28 million frames); sort over 10000 passes in
  8.5 s under 131072 frames already; the C stack bounded none of them.
  The depth needed is the eight frames per element: 10000 pass and 16000
  fail under 131072; 20000 pass and 32000 fail under 262144.  Decision
  taken instead: the flag recursion_limit of the Python backend, the
  recursion limit of the interpreter while a value is computed
  (generate_values), 262144 by default (about thirty thousand elements
  under length, 175 MB at the limit, about 4 KB per nested element), an
  int or None (the limit of the interpreter stays); the C++ backend
  ignores it, as the Python backend ignores stack_limit.  An alternative
  that reaches the limit is handled as before (overflow rotation and the
  drop of a stuck one).  unit_py_fairness.py sets 1 << 14 for its tests,
  so its lists of ten thousand elements still overflow in about a second
  (6 s for the file, as before).  tests/unit_long_lists.py now passes
  length and sort over ten thousand elements on both backends (py 6.3 s,
  the sort is 5 s of it) and pins the flag: under 1 << 14 the same length
  raises RecursionError and a thousand elements pass.  Docs: the flag in
  interpreter/flags.py, the sprite-exec page (Selecting a Backend and
  Bounding One Alternative) and the EnvironmentVariables page; the
  C-stack sentence is withdrawn.  The issue gets no comment from this
  stream (approval needed); the text is in the report.
